WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:11.953
- Good afternoon, and welcome.

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:16.690
Good afternoon, welcome to you,

00:00:16.690 --> 00:00:20.200
and thanks for coming to this lecture,

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:21.780
presented by the Franke Program

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:23.443
in Science and the Humanities.

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:27.450
Some of you are familiar with the program,

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:30.200
and others of you are visiting Yale,

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:32.350
perhaps for the first
time in a long while.

00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:34.023
Welcome to Yale.

00:00:34.023 --> 00:00:37.200
A hearty and warm welcome to
the Whitney Humanities Center

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:39.620
on this glorious day,

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:42.030
which much of New England is embedded

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:43.470
in a blizzard right now,

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:46.893
but we seem to have dodged
the bullet, as it were.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:51.000
The Franke Program in
Science and the Humanities

00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:55.820
brings together diverse
audiences to explore topics

00:00:56.965 --> 00:00:59.880
at the interface between
science, technology,

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:02.280
and the broader university,

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:05.910
the broader areas of knowledges
focusing on the humanities,

00:01:05.910 --> 00:01:08.170
so we're excited to have you here today.

00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:11.050
I want to, as long as we
have such a robust audience,

00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:13.960
I'm gonna advertise our events
coming up in the spring,

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:16.010
lots more of excitement.

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:18.050
In particular, we'll be cohosting,

00:01:18.050 --> 00:01:19.800
with the Whitney Humanities Center,

00:01:21.130 --> 00:01:22.350
the Shulman Lecture Series,

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:25.510
organized around a course
called Other Minds,

00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:29.890
about cognitive science of
humans and animals comparatively.

00:01:29.890 --> 00:01:31.420
They're sponsoring a series of lectures,

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:34.020
one this week, on February 15th,

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:36.620
by Alexandra Horowitz from Barnard College

00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:38.830
on what is it like to be a dog?

00:01:38.830 --> 00:01:41.640
On dog cognition.

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:45.290
February 22nd, Peter
Godfrey-Smith will be speaking

00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:47.930
on Paths to the Evolution
of Consciousness.

00:01:47.930 --> 00:01:49.353
On April 5th,

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:53.750
Mark Moffett of the
Smithsonian, an ant biologist,

00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:54.660
will be speaking on

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:57.570
Social Identity in Ant
and Human Societies,

00:01:57.570 --> 00:02:01.660
and on April 26, Anne Harrington
from Harvard psychology

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:03.900
will be speaking of Mindful Minds,

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:06.153
America's Love Affair with Meditation.

00:02:07.330 --> 00:02:09.610
The other events cosponsored or sponsored

00:02:11.024 --> 00:02:13.910
by the Franke Program
coming up in the spring,

00:02:13.910 --> 00:02:15.920
on February 24th, we will cosponsor,

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:18.865
with the History of Science
and Medicine program,

00:02:18.865 --> 00:02:21.200
a talk by Dr. Sam Roberts,

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:22.830
director of the Columbia University

00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:25.790
Institute for Research in
African American Studies

00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:27.600
and associate professor of history

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:28.990
and sociomedical sciences.

00:02:28.990 --> 00:02:30.760
He'll be speaking in a conference

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:34.440
on Critical Histories
and Activist Futures:

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:36.760
Science, Medicine, and Racial Violence

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:38.390
at the Yale Medical School.

00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:40.470
That's at 5:00 p.m.

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:43.140
We're also, on Friday, March 3rd,

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:45.860
we'll be cosponsoring a conference

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:47.590
with the American Studies program and the

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:51.870
Center for Race, Indigeneity,
and Transnational Migration

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:52.703
here at Yale,

00:02:52.703 --> 00:02:55.340
a conference on Mapping
for Social Justice,

00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:58.510
Approaches with New Media
and Digital Humanities,

00:02:58.510 --> 00:03:01.830
with speakers from eight
different universities

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:05.040
visiting for that whole-day program.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:07.713
On April 10th, we have Andrea Wulf,

00:03:08.690 --> 00:03:11.830
who will be speaking on
"The Invention of Nature,"

00:03:11.830 --> 00:03:14.880
her award-winning biography
of Alexander Von Humboldt,

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:19.130
a spectacular book and,
I'm sure, an exciting talk.

00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:23.450
On Monday, April 17th,
we have Dr. Hasok Chang,

00:03:23.450 --> 00:03:25.090
historian and philosopher of science

00:03:25.090 --> 00:03:27.110
from Cambridge University,

00:03:27.110 --> 00:03:30.590
speaking on Is There Room
for Pluralism in Science?

00:03:30.590 --> 00:03:34.250
That will be here in the Whitney
at 4:00 p.m. on the 17th,

00:03:34.250 --> 00:03:36.470
and then, on April 22nd,

00:03:36.470 --> 00:03:40.660
we cosponsor with the Yale
Environmental History program,

00:03:40.660 --> 00:03:44.530
a conference on New Perspectives
in Environmental History.

00:03:44.530 --> 00:03:48.493
That'll be in Luce Hall
all day on April 22nd,

00:03:49.810 --> 00:03:50.950
so before we get started,

00:03:50.950 --> 00:03:53.750
I wanna thank, of course, Ty Kamp,

00:03:53.750 --> 00:03:56.960
assistant director here
in the Franke Program,

00:03:56.960 --> 00:03:59.870
and also, the Whitney
Humanities Center staff,

00:03:59.870 --> 00:04:03.940
always supporting our
activities marvelously,

00:04:03.940 --> 00:04:06.770
so today, it's my pleasure to introduce

00:04:06.770 --> 00:04:09.440
Dr. Robin Wall Kimmerer,

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:13.560
who is a moss biologist,
ecologist, and an acclaimed writer.

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:15.940
She is a SUNY distinguished
teaching professor

00:04:15.940 --> 00:04:19.910
at the SUNY College of
Environmental Science and Forestry

00:04:19.910 --> 00:04:21.610
in Syracuse, New York.

00:04:21.610 --> 00:04:23.610
She was also founding
director at the college

00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:27.560
of the Center for Native
Peoples and the Environment,

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:29.330
the mission of which is to create programs

00:04:29.330 --> 00:04:30.920
which draw on the wisdom

00:04:30.920 --> 00:04:34.700
of both Indigenous and
scientific knowledge

00:04:34.700 --> 00:04:37.710
for the shared goal of sustainability.

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:39.150
Professor Kimmerer's research

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:41.930
includes reproductive ecology, dispersal,

00:04:41.930 --> 00:04:43.820
and community ecology of mosses

00:04:43.820 --> 00:04:45.550
in Northeastern North America,

00:04:45.550 --> 00:04:46.830
and the role, more recently,

00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:48.950
the role of traditional
ecological knowledge

00:04:48.950 --> 00:04:50.763
in ecological restoration.

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:53.830
In collaboration with tribal partners,

00:04:53.830 --> 00:04:57.070
she and her students have
an active research program

00:04:57.070 --> 00:04:59.170
in the ecology and restoration of plants

00:04:59.170 --> 00:05:02.130
of cultural significance to Native people.

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:04.890
Professor Kimmerer is a member
of the Potawatomi Nation,

00:05:04.890 --> 00:05:07.680
and she is the cofounder
and past president

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:10.430
of the Traditional
Ecological Knowledge Section

00:05:10.430 --> 00:05:13.670
of the Ecological Society of America.

00:05:13.670 --> 00:05:15.510
Professor Kimmerer is the author

00:05:15.510 --> 00:05:17.250
of numerous scientific papers

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:19.790
on ecology of moss, restoration ecology,

00:05:19.790 --> 00:05:22.570
and on contributions to
traditional ecology knowledge

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:26.070
to understanding our natural world.

00:05:26.070 --> 00:05:28.400
She is also the author

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:29.760
of a number of acclaimed books,

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:31.740
including "Gathering Moss,"

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:32.940
which is an intimate account

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:35.297
of the biology and lives of mosses.

00:05:35.297 --> 00:05:36.700
"Gathering Moss" was awarded

00:05:36.700 --> 00:05:38.580
the prestigious John Burroughs Medal

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:42.210
for nature writing in 2005.

00:05:42.210 --> 00:05:45.217
Her book "Braiding Sweetgrass:
"Indigenous Wisdom,

00:05:45.217 --> 00:05:47.840
"Scientific Knowledge, and
the Teaching of Plants"

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:49.240
was also awarded

00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:53.250
the Sigurd Olson Nature
Writing Award in 2013.

00:05:53.250 --> 00:05:55.170
Throughout her writing,

00:05:55.170 --> 00:05:58.520
Robin Kimmerer uses her
elegant and visionary voice

00:05:58.520 --> 00:05:59.800
to tell intimate stories

00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:03.180
about small, forgotten,
or overlooked communities,

00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:06.760
from the tiny assemblages
of mosses in the forest

00:06:06.760 --> 00:06:10.290
to the original Indigenous
nations of this continent.

00:06:10.290 --> 00:06:12.523
Join me in welcoming Robin Wall Kimmerer.

00:06:12.523 --> 00:06:15.690
(audience applauding)

00:06:25.938 --> 00:06:29.459
Before we get started, I
wanna also let you know

00:06:29.459 --> 00:06:31.800
that there'll be a
reception after the talk

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:34.620
directly down the hallway
at the end of the hallway,

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:35.990
so please stay around

00:06:37.242 --> 00:06:40.290
and join us for that
continued conversation.

00:06:40.290 --> 00:06:41.310
- Thank you so much.

00:06:41.310 --> 00:06:43.120
Thank you all for being here,

00:06:43.120 --> 00:06:47.070
for taking the time to come
together and share ideas.

00:06:47.070 --> 00:06:50.460
Thanks to the Franke Center
for this wonderful invitation,

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:53.180
and having invited a plant ecologist

00:06:53.180 --> 00:06:55.120
to come and speak with you,

00:06:55.120 --> 00:06:58.653
you might be thinking we're
gonna see some data today.

00:06:59.596 --> 00:07:00.860
Yeah, nope.

00:07:00.860 --> 00:07:04.740
I love a good P value as
much as the next scientist,

00:07:04.740 --> 00:07:07.440
but the kind of values we're
gonna be talking about today

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:10.040
lie at the intersection
of nature and culture.

00:07:10.040 --> 00:07:12.480
We're really gonna be
looking at human values,

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:16.120
and instead of graphs and tables,

00:07:16.120 --> 00:07:17.050
we're really gonna use

00:07:17.050 --> 00:07:18.940
what my friend John Tallmadge has called

00:07:18.940 --> 00:07:23.290
the most sophisticated
communication technology

00:07:23.290 --> 00:07:24.510
ever devised,

00:07:24.510 --> 00:07:27.330
and my students always
say, "You mean Google?"

00:07:27.330 --> 00:07:30.430
And no, I say, "I mean the story,"

00:07:30.430 --> 00:07:33.880
so we're gonna talk stories today,

00:07:33.880 --> 00:07:38.730
but first, I wanna greet
you in my language.

00:07:38.730 --> 00:07:40.560
I don't speak much of our language,

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:42.460
but our teachers and our Elders tell us

00:07:42.460 --> 00:07:45.220
that because our language
is an endangered species,

00:07:45.220 --> 00:07:48.800
we really need to breathe
life into it whenever we can

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:50.060
and use it,

00:07:50.060 --> 00:07:52.382
so in the Potawatomi
language, I greet you.

00:07:52.382 --> 00:07:55.521
I say (speaking in Potawatomi).

00:07:55.521 --> 00:07:56.624
(audience speaking in Potawatomi)

00:07:56.624 --> 00:08:00.541
(Robin speaking in Potawatomi)

00:08:07.340 --> 00:08:10.440
I've told you that I
am a Potawatomi woman,

00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:12.287
that my name is (speaking in Potawatomi),

00:08:13.290 --> 00:08:15.570
the Light Shining through Sky Woman.

00:08:15.570 --> 00:08:18.730
I'm a member of the Bear
Clan and also of the Eagles,

00:08:18.730 --> 00:08:21.633
and I'm very grateful to be here with you,

00:08:23.320 --> 00:08:27.220
and gratitude is our first responsibility,

00:08:27.220 --> 00:08:30.490
and so let's just take a
moment to begin with gratitude,

00:08:30.490 --> 00:08:32.520
to remember that this morning,

00:08:32.520 --> 00:08:36.180
when we woke up, we put
our feet on Mother Earth,

00:08:36.180 --> 00:08:39.970
and everything that we
needed was provided for us,

00:08:39.970 --> 00:08:41.490
that we had water to drink.

00:08:41.490 --> 00:08:44.820
We had beautiful clouds
moving across the sky

00:08:44.820 --> 00:08:46.900
with that beautiful air.

00:08:46.900 --> 00:08:48.120
We had food.

00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:50.930
We had the companionship
of geese in the sky

00:08:50.930 --> 00:08:54.000
and the companionship of
the bare-branched maples

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:57.410
and the companionship of each other,

00:08:57.410 --> 00:08:59.950
so many gifts that come to us,

00:08:59.950 --> 00:09:04.020
and our first responsibility,
then, is gratitude,

00:09:04.020 --> 00:09:07.130
and I also want to give
a special gratitude

00:09:07.130 --> 00:09:09.140
to the original peoples of this place,

00:09:09.140 --> 00:09:12.990
the Pequot peoples, on
whose ancestral territory

00:09:12.990 --> 00:09:15.633
we are sitting at this very moment.

00:09:17.070 --> 00:09:21.500
To introduce today's talk
about the teachings of plants,

00:09:21.500 --> 00:09:24.290
I want to introduce you to
one of my great teachers,

00:09:24.290 --> 00:09:27.333
this plant called (speaks
in Potawatomi), sweetgrass.

00:09:28.280 --> 00:09:30.230
Sweetgrass, in our language,

00:09:30.230 --> 00:09:33.660
that word, (speaks in Potawatomi),
actually means something

00:09:33.660 --> 00:09:35.710
about the beautiful
fragrance of that plant.

00:09:35.710 --> 00:09:38.350
Has a lovely sort of vanilla odor to it.

00:09:38.350 --> 00:09:42.850
It means the opened wet
meadows where it grows,

00:09:42.850 --> 00:09:47.230
and it also means a call to
ceremony and a call to prayer

00:09:47.230 --> 00:09:49.470
because this is a sacred plant for us.

00:09:49.470 --> 00:09:51.210
This is a medicine plant.

00:09:51.210 --> 00:09:53.510
It simultaneously heals the earth

00:09:53.510 --> 00:09:56.920
because it's an early
successional rhizomatous perennial

00:09:56.920 --> 00:09:59.650
that binds up disturbed soil,

00:09:59.650 --> 00:10:04.310
but it is also a healer of spirit as well,

00:10:04.310 --> 00:10:07.400
so this is an important medicine plant,

00:10:07.400 --> 00:10:09.740
and while we call it
(speaks in Potawatomi),

00:10:09.740 --> 00:10:11.750
you may know it as sweetgrass,

00:10:11.750 --> 00:10:15.850
Linnaeus called it Hierochloe odorata,

00:10:15.850 --> 00:10:17.750
and in botanical Latin,

00:10:17.750 --> 00:10:22.080
that means the sacred,
fragrant, holy grass,

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:25.680
so I think Linnaeus knew
this plant very well.

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:28.980
We'll get back to Linnaeus
in just a little bit.

00:10:28.980 --> 00:10:31.590
When you see a (speaks
in Potawatomi) like this,

00:10:31.590 --> 00:10:34.420
you almost always see sweetgrass braided.

00:10:34.420 --> 00:10:36.163
Anybody know why that is?

00:10:38.124 --> 00:10:40.090
- [Audience Member] To make it stronger.

00:10:40.090 --> 00:10:42.550
- It is certainly stronger.

00:10:42.550 --> 00:10:45.120
The reason that we braid sweetgrass

00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:48.260
is that sweetgrass is part
of our creation stories,

00:10:48.260 --> 00:10:50.070
and that sweetgrass is understood

00:10:50.070 --> 00:10:52.230
as one of the first plants
that grows on earth,

00:10:52.230 --> 00:10:55.643
and we think of it as
the hair of Mother Earth,

00:10:56.730 --> 00:10:58.530
and so now you know why we braid it.

00:10:59.790 --> 00:11:02.510
Think about the last time
you braided someone's hair

00:11:04.060 --> 00:11:05.703
or someone braided your hair,

00:11:06.750 --> 00:11:10.760
and the gentle smiles that
I just saw emerge here

00:11:10.760 --> 00:11:13.010
tell us why we braid sweetgrass

00:11:13.010 --> 00:11:16.520
because it's a form of
relationship, isn't it, braiding?

00:11:16.520 --> 00:11:19.770
We braid Mother Earth's hair
in the form of sweetgrass

00:11:19.770 --> 00:11:22.248
as a sign of our tender care for her,

00:11:22.248 --> 00:11:24.650
and so this important plant

00:11:24.650 --> 00:11:27.280
comes to us as a braid,

00:11:27.280 --> 00:11:30.693
and we'll have more to say
about that in a little bit.

00:11:31.700 --> 00:11:35.290
This notion of braiding
sweetgrass, those three strands,

00:11:35.290 --> 00:11:38.040
I also use as the dominant metaphor

00:11:38.040 --> 00:11:39.810
in the book "Braiding Sweetgrass,"

00:11:39.810 --> 00:11:41.950
thinking about each one of those strands,

00:11:41.950 --> 00:11:46.800
of those three strands, one
as scientific knowledge,

00:11:46.800 --> 00:11:50.654
one of them as Indigenous
traditional knowledge,

00:11:50.654 --> 00:11:54.050
and the third, the knowledge
of the plants themselves

00:11:54.050 --> 00:11:57.140
because both traditional
knowledge and scientific knowledge

00:11:57.140 --> 00:11:59.560
are constructed knowledge by humans,

00:11:59.560 --> 00:12:01.010
but we also have to acknowledge

00:12:01.010 --> 00:12:04.480
the inherent wisdom and
knowledge that are in the plants,

00:12:04.480 --> 00:12:07.580
and so that too is
represented in this braid,

00:12:07.580 --> 00:12:10.100
all these ways of knowing,

00:12:10.100 --> 00:12:12.080
but I have to tell you
that the relationship,

00:12:12.080 --> 00:12:14.410
for me, as a Native scientist,

00:12:14.410 --> 00:12:16.250
of those three ways of knowing

00:12:16.250 --> 00:12:21.250
has not always been an easy
one, as you might imagine,

00:12:21.300 --> 00:12:24.480
and let me just tell you
a little story about that.

00:12:24.480 --> 00:12:27.820
Here you see, of course,
beautiful goldenrod and asters,

00:12:27.820 --> 00:12:29.140
that purple and yellow.

00:12:29.140 --> 00:12:32.200
They're so stunning together in the fall,

00:12:32.200 --> 00:12:35.390
and I always loved this
combination of plants,

00:12:35.390 --> 00:12:37.650
and I was born a botanist.

00:12:37.650 --> 00:12:38.840
I can't remember a time

00:12:38.840 --> 00:12:42.340
when I wasn't attracted to
and engaged with plants,

00:12:42.340 --> 00:12:46.710
and this particular pair
has always been my favorite,

00:12:46.710 --> 00:12:49.530
so when it came time to go to college,

00:12:49.530 --> 00:12:51.660
I knew I had to study botany,

00:12:51.660 --> 00:12:56.020
and when I went to school,
on my very first day,

00:12:56.020 --> 00:12:57.597
my freshmen advisor said to me,

00:12:57.597 --> 00:13:01.340
"So Miss Wall, why do you
want to be a botanist?"

00:13:01.340 --> 00:13:04.097
And I said, "I want to know

00:13:04.097 --> 00:13:06.477
"why goldenrod and New England asters

00:13:06.477 --> 00:13:08.548
"look beautiful together."

00:13:08.548 --> 00:13:09.870
(audience member chuckles)

00:13:09.870 --> 00:13:13.400
That's just what he did. (laughs)

00:13:13.400 --> 00:13:15.310
I wanted to know about relationship.

00:13:15.310 --> 00:13:18.440
I wanted to know about harmony and beauty

00:13:18.440 --> 00:13:21.290
because these plants do
look so stunning together,

00:13:21.290 --> 00:13:23.540
and they often grow intermixed,

00:13:23.540 --> 00:13:27.030
so they're aesthetically so gorgeous,

00:13:27.030 --> 00:13:27.863
and I thought

00:13:27.863 --> 00:13:31.480
there must be ecological
meaning to that as well,

00:13:31.480 --> 00:13:36.290
but he said, (clears throat)
"My dear, that is not science,"

00:13:36.290 --> 00:13:40.150
and so, as a person very much interested

00:13:40.150 --> 00:13:42.650
in traditional Indigenous ways of knowing,

00:13:42.650 --> 00:13:45.987
I said, "Well, I also want to
know why plants make medicine

00:13:45.987 --> 00:13:48.477
"and why you can bend willows for baskets

00:13:48.477 --> 00:13:50.550
"and not other plants,"

00:13:50.550 --> 00:13:53.220
and he said, "Also not science."

00:13:53.220 --> 00:13:55.357
He said, "If you want
to know those things,

00:13:55.357 --> 00:13:57.990
"you shouldn't be in science,"

00:13:57.990 --> 00:14:01.610
and so this was my first encounter

00:14:01.610 --> 00:14:03.660
with the scientific worldview,

00:14:03.660 --> 00:14:05.720
coming from an Indigenous worldview

00:14:05.720 --> 00:14:08.550
of growing up on the land in my community,

00:14:08.550 --> 00:14:11.115
and that's why I look so happy (laughs)

00:14:11.115 --> 00:14:11.948
(audience laughing)

00:14:11.948 --> 00:14:14.113
in my freshman photo.

00:14:16.370 --> 00:14:18.430
I was stunned, quite honestly,

00:14:18.430 --> 00:14:20.867
because I thought a question of beauty

00:14:20.867 --> 00:14:22.890
was a darn good question,

00:14:22.890 --> 00:14:25.180
but I was told it wasn't,
that it didn't matter,

00:14:25.180 --> 00:14:26.910
and that if I was interested in beauty,

00:14:26.910 --> 00:14:29.240
I should have gone to art school,

00:14:29.240 --> 00:14:32.580
and I had no vocabulary for resistance.

00:14:32.580 --> 00:14:34.210
I didn't know what to do about this.

00:14:34.210 --> 00:14:36.310
I thought that I must've made a mistake

00:14:36.310 --> 00:14:40.600
about what was important to me
and interesting about plants,

00:14:40.600 --> 00:14:44.120
and so he signed me up for
my general botany classes,

00:14:44.120 --> 00:14:46.753
and I went right in to take them,

00:14:48.460 --> 00:14:51.460
but now, thinking back on that day

00:14:51.460 --> 00:14:55.680
and my gleeful look on my first
day getting to study botany,

00:14:55.680 --> 00:14:57.700
which was my dream come true,

00:14:57.700 --> 00:15:01.520
was that it was really
a very powerful echo

00:15:01.520 --> 00:15:04.323
of my grandfather's first day at school,

00:15:05.800 --> 00:15:08.340
and my grandfather's first day of school

00:15:08.340 --> 00:15:10.410
was when he was nine years old,

00:15:10.410 --> 00:15:13.550
when he was taken from our
reservation in Oklahoma

00:15:13.550 --> 00:15:17.003
and put on a train and sent
to the Carlisle Indian School.

00:15:18.460 --> 00:15:23.060
His first day of school, kinda like mine,

00:15:23.060 --> 00:15:25.727
where we were told, "What
you think doesn't matter.

00:15:25.727 --> 00:15:28.777
"Your way of knowing, there's
no room for that here,"

00:15:30.040 --> 00:15:32.173
but they didn't cut my hair,

00:15:33.180 --> 00:15:38.180
and I could still speak
my own language, kind of,

00:15:38.340 --> 00:15:42.460
because my language of
nature was nature as subject.

00:15:42.460 --> 00:15:44.790
I understood the plants and the animals,

00:15:44.790 --> 00:15:47.040
the insects, the birds around me

00:15:47.040 --> 00:15:49.720
as beings, as persons,

00:15:49.720 --> 00:15:54.720
and that the land, which I was
being told was the ecosystem,

00:15:54.960 --> 00:15:56.760
I understood as a community,

00:15:56.760 --> 00:16:00.200
a community of sovereign beings,

00:16:00.200 --> 00:16:02.173
and with that worldview,

00:16:02.173 --> 00:16:03.006
I have to tell you

00:16:03.006 --> 00:16:04.950
that studying botany
was really challenging

00:16:04.950 --> 00:16:06.880
because the organisms
that I had thought of

00:16:06.880 --> 00:16:08.820
as persons, in a sense,

00:16:08.820 --> 00:16:12.580
were now reduced to objects,

00:16:12.580 --> 00:16:14.500
and, in fact, this was the model

00:16:14.500 --> 00:16:17.640
that we were given of ecology,

00:16:17.640 --> 00:16:19.500
that this is what an ecosystem was.

00:16:19.500 --> 00:16:23.190
The ecosystem as machine,
not as community.

00:16:23.190 --> 00:16:24.150
I thought I had wandered

00:16:24.150 --> 00:16:27.400
into an engineering class by mistake,

00:16:27.400 --> 00:16:29.630
nature as object,

00:16:29.630 --> 00:16:32.660
because, again, within the
Indigenous way of thinking,

00:16:32.660 --> 00:16:34.230
I was raised to understand

00:16:34.230 --> 00:16:37.230
that every person has these four ways,

00:16:37.230 --> 00:16:40.690
depicted in our medicine wheel,
of understanding the world:

00:16:40.690 --> 00:16:43.410
mind, body, emotion, and spirit,

00:16:43.410 --> 00:16:46.830
and we're always told
that unless you understand

00:16:46.830 --> 00:16:49.340
with all of these four
gifts of human beings,

00:16:49.340 --> 00:16:51.920
you don't really understand it,

00:16:51.920 --> 00:16:54.560
but in going to the scientific worldview,

00:16:54.560 --> 00:16:58.430
I went to a worldview that
just privileges the intellect

00:16:58.430 --> 00:17:02.000
and that which can be observed
and measured by science,

00:17:02.000 --> 00:17:07.000
but that very deliberately
sets aside spirit and emotion,

00:17:07.010 --> 00:17:09.530
those other gifts that we have.

00:17:09.530 --> 00:17:13.350
I had walked out of a worldview

00:17:13.350 --> 00:17:17.640
in which land is understood
as a web of relationship

00:17:17.640 --> 00:17:20.050
that brings all those things together.

00:17:20.050 --> 00:17:21.920
What we didn't really understand,

00:17:21.920 --> 00:17:26.000
I think, at that point was,
you mentioned pluralism,

00:17:26.000 --> 00:17:26.833
that you have a speaker

00:17:26.833 --> 00:17:29.790
coming to talk about pluralism in science,

00:17:29.790 --> 00:17:34.410
that just as we know that the
driver of biological evolution

00:17:34.410 --> 00:17:36.420
is genetic diversity,

00:17:36.420 --> 00:17:39.350
that if we think about cultural evolution

00:17:39.350 --> 00:17:41.170
toward sustainability,

00:17:41.170 --> 00:17:43.610
toward right relationship with the earth,

00:17:43.610 --> 00:17:45.780
that that requires intellectual diversity.

00:17:45.780 --> 00:17:48.140
It requires all kinds of ways

00:17:48.140 --> 00:17:50.800
of understanding our relationships,

00:17:50.800 --> 00:17:53.600
and that is really the
work that we're doing

00:17:53.600 --> 00:17:56.120
at the Center for Native
Peoples and the Environment

00:17:56.120 --> 00:18:00.130
is to bring together
the wisdom and practices

00:18:00.130 --> 00:18:03.140
of traditional Indigenous
environmental philosophy,

00:18:03.140 --> 00:18:05.670
paired with the tools of Western science

00:18:05.670 --> 00:18:09.970
to solve our most pressing
environmental problems,

00:18:09.970 --> 00:18:14.180
and we like to think of it
not so much as a monoculture.

00:18:14.180 --> 00:18:15.300
I don't know about you,

00:18:15.300 --> 00:18:18.980
but what I experienced as
a brand-new college student

00:18:18.980 --> 00:18:20.900
was an intellectual monoculture,

00:18:20.900 --> 00:18:23.440
that the way to understand nature

00:18:23.440 --> 00:18:26.240
was through the scientific lens only,

00:18:26.240 --> 00:18:27.890
but what we're thinking about doing,

00:18:27.890 --> 00:18:30.440
and we're trying to
practice in our center,

00:18:30.440 --> 00:18:32.740
is to create a knowledge garden

00:18:32.740 --> 00:18:35.760
inspired by the Three Sisters polyculture

00:18:35.760 --> 00:18:38.510
grown by Native peoples
all over the continent,

00:18:38.510 --> 00:18:41.090
where you know that
corn, beans, and squash

00:18:41.090 --> 00:18:42.950
are not grown in isolation,

00:18:42.950 --> 00:18:45.410
but they're grown together, right?

00:18:45.410 --> 00:18:48.260
And it reduces competition between them.

00:18:48.260 --> 00:18:49.110
They partner.

00:18:49.110 --> 00:18:51.490
They support one another,

00:18:51.490 --> 00:18:55.780
but the beans don't
become the squash, right?

00:18:55.780 --> 00:19:00.080
There's not a privileging of
one over the other, a blending.

00:19:00.080 --> 00:19:03.600
We have sovereign ways of
knowing that support each other,

00:19:03.600 --> 00:19:05.170
and so the garden,

00:19:05.170 --> 00:19:07.580
the knowledge garden that
we are trying to create

00:19:07.580 --> 00:19:10.480
is this one, where we have a symbiosis

00:19:10.480 --> 00:19:13.310
between traditional ecological knowledge

00:19:13.310 --> 00:19:14.840
and Western science,

00:19:14.840 --> 00:19:19.460
and then ourselves, as teachers,
as educators, as thinkers

00:19:19.460 --> 00:19:22.560
to help create that symbiosis.

00:19:22.560 --> 00:19:25.193
This is what we are trying to do,

00:19:26.510 --> 00:19:30.830
so if we go back here to this
notion of nature as object

00:19:30.830 --> 00:19:34.120
versus nature as subject,

00:19:34.120 --> 00:19:38.860
this question of the nature
of nature, if you will,

00:19:38.860 --> 00:19:41.960
is certainly not a new one,

00:19:41.960 --> 00:19:44.820
and what I wanna talk
to you about today is

00:19:44.820 --> 00:19:49.150
the notion of object
versus subject in plants

00:19:49.150 --> 00:19:52.090
and thinking about plants as persons

00:19:52.090 --> 00:19:55.630
and what we gain in that dialogue.

00:19:55.630 --> 00:19:57.570
Two books that I wanna recommend to you,

00:19:57.570 --> 00:20:00.330
if this is of any interest to you at all,

00:20:00.330 --> 00:20:01.227
these wonderful books,

00:20:01.227 --> 00:20:04.960
"The Philosopher's Plant"
and "Plants as Persons,"

00:20:04.960 --> 00:20:07.050
are really great treatises

00:20:07.050 --> 00:20:10.290
that have informed a lot of my thinking,

00:20:10.290 --> 00:20:15.290
and we will begin, as we
do in the Western canon,

00:20:15.530 --> 00:20:17.500
with the classics, right?

00:20:17.500 --> 00:20:18.880
The Greek philosophers

00:20:18.880 --> 00:20:22.220
who thought about the
nature of other beings

00:20:22.220 --> 00:20:25.070
and whether or not
plants were living beings

00:20:25.070 --> 00:20:29.120
was an active area of discussion,

00:20:29.120 --> 00:20:31.510
and many of the early philosophers,

00:20:31.510 --> 00:20:32.870
Plato and Theophrastus,

00:20:32.870 --> 00:20:35.960
who was sort of the father of botany,

00:20:35.960 --> 00:20:37.390
concluded that while plants

00:20:37.390 --> 00:20:40.150
were very, very different from animals,

00:20:40.150 --> 00:20:42.760
they were definitely living things.

00:20:42.760 --> 00:20:44.420
They were not as close to rocks

00:20:44.420 --> 00:20:47.530
as many of their
contemporaries had thought,

00:20:47.530 --> 00:20:51.270
and they actually said
that plants have souls.

00:20:51.270 --> 00:20:53.420
They have virtuous souls,

00:20:53.420 --> 00:20:55.770
which are associated with their beauty,

00:20:55.770 --> 00:20:57.350
with their generosity,

00:20:57.350 --> 00:21:00.730
and with their provisioning
for everybody else,

00:21:00.730 --> 00:21:04.130
but Aristotle suggested otherwise.

00:21:04.130 --> 00:21:05.597
I love this quote:

00:21:05.597 --> 00:21:07.637
"Some have said that
plants have intelligence

00:21:07.637 --> 00:21:09.327
"and can acquire knowledge.

00:21:09.327 --> 00:21:11.817
"Let us dismiss these theories as trivial

00:21:11.817 --> 00:21:15.070
"and abide by sound reasoning."

00:21:15.070 --> 00:21:19.800
He thought that if plants had any notion

00:21:19.800 --> 00:21:21.300
of soul or being,

00:21:21.300 --> 00:21:23.770
this is what he called a vegetal soul,

00:21:23.770 --> 00:21:25.500
and a vegetal soul simply meant

00:21:25.500 --> 00:21:28.850
that they had enough going on to grow,

00:21:28.850 --> 00:21:32.390
to grow and to reproduce, but that's it,

00:21:32.390 --> 00:21:34.893
and, of course, you think
in the scala naturae,

00:21:35.771 --> 00:21:37.230
on the great chain of being,

00:21:37.230 --> 00:21:39.440
where do plants occur?

00:21:39.440 --> 00:21:40.870
Way down at the bottom

00:21:40.870 --> 00:21:44.240
of this hierarchical ranked system, right?

00:21:44.240 --> 00:21:48.710
In which the angels are on the
top, humans just below them,

00:21:48.710 --> 00:21:53.460
and plants just above the rocks,

00:21:53.460 --> 00:21:57.210
and this notion of plants as primitive,

00:21:57.210 --> 00:21:59.880
the hierarchical ranking of beings

00:21:59.880 --> 00:22:04.000
certainly underpins much of
our botanical sciences today,

00:22:04.000 --> 00:22:07.340
as we'll see in just a second,

00:22:07.340 --> 00:22:10.340
so this Western worldview,

00:22:10.340 --> 00:22:12.170
placement of plants

00:22:12.170 --> 00:22:15.660
as low man on the totem pole, right?

00:22:15.660 --> 00:22:18.860
That they are at the bottom
of biological hierarchies,

00:22:18.860 --> 00:22:21.763
primitive, lacking in most capacities.

00:22:23.000 --> 00:22:25.270
While I would say that that idea

00:22:25.270 --> 00:22:26.920
of low man on the totem pole

00:22:26.920 --> 00:22:31.100
needs some critique in
a number of regards,

00:22:31.100 --> 00:22:32.810
particularly what does it mean

00:22:32.810 --> 00:22:35.573
to be low man on the totem pole?

00:22:36.690 --> 00:22:39.560
A common misperception by Westerners

00:22:39.560 --> 00:22:44.440
looking at this marvelous art
of the Pacific Northwest coast

00:22:44.440 --> 00:22:47.150
is that the one who is on top

00:22:47.150 --> 00:22:49.220
of the totem pole

00:22:49.220 --> 00:22:52.823
is the most powerful and the most honored.

00:22:54.470 --> 00:22:55.520
That's pretty consistent

00:22:55.520 --> 00:22:57.780
with our biological hierarchies, right?

00:22:57.780 --> 00:23:01.030
Where we are at the really
top, but that's not true.

00:23:01.030 --> 00:23:02.060
In this art form,

00:23:02.060 --> 00:23:05.430
the most influential, the
one on which everyone relies,

00:23:05.430 --> 00:23:08.150
is at the bottom of the totem pole,

00:23:08.150 --> 00:23:09.880
perhaps right where plants are,

00:23:09.880 --> 00:23:13.410
the most influential and important

00:23:13.410 --> 00:23:15.870
taking their place right there.

00:23:15.870 --> 00:23:18.220
Like the plants at the
base of the food chain,

00:23:18.220 --> 00:23:21.113
they hold everything else up,

00:23:22.850 --> 00:23:24.260
and in contrast

00:23:24.260 --> 00:23:27.510
to this hierarchical way
of thinking about plants

00:23:27.510 --> 00:23:31.070
with plants at the bottom
and humans at the top,

00:23:31.070 --> 00:23:33.040
in the Indigenous worldview,

00:23:33.040 --> 00:23:36.300
from the dreamtime of
Aboriginal Australians

00:23:36.300 --> 00:23:38.740
to my own Potawatomi people,

00:23:38.740 --> 00:23:43.740
the plants are viewed as
persons, as full-fledged persons,

00:23:43.990 --> 00:23:46.930
with their own gifts,
their own intelligences,

00:23:46.930 --> 00:23:51.930
their own intentions, their
own ways of being in the world,

00:23:52.300 --> 00:23:57.030
and when I say that plants
are understood as persons,

00:23:57.030 --> 00:24:00.170
I don't mean this kind of
person, a fictional person,

00:24:00.170 --> 00:24:04.340
like the familiar Ents of Fangorn Forest,

00:24:04.340 --> 00:24:07.570
and I certainly don't mean these persons.

00:24:07.570 --> 00:24:08.403
Isn't it interesting?

00:24:08.403 --> 00:24:10.630
All you have to do is put a face on things

00:24:10.630 --> 00:24:13.270
and suddenly they become beings.

00:24:13.270 --> 00:24:18.253
Anthropomorphism at its very
best: a smile on a carrot.

00:24:19.500 --> 00:24:22.270
The kind of personhood that I mean,

00:24:22.270 --> 00:24:25.470
as a reflection of
Indigenous ways of knowing,

00:24:25.470 --> 00:24:27.270
is not anthropomorphic.

00:24:27.270 --> 00:24:31.720
It is not assigning human characters

00:24:31.720 --> 00:24:33.760
to plants at all.

00:24:33.760 --> 00:24:37.300
In fact, what it does
is to celebrate the fact

00:24:37.300 --> 00:24:39.400
that plants are very different,

00:24:39.400 --> 00:24:43.060
very different ways of
being than humankind,

00:24:43.060 --> 00:24:45.670
but they, too, are persons.

00:24:45.670 --> 00:24:49.750
They, too, are autonomous beings
with their own experiences,

00:24:49.750 --> 00:24:51.130
their own perceptions,

00:24:51.130 --> 00:24:54.390
and indeed, their own agency in the world.

00:24:54.390 --> 00:24:56.740
A person can have intelligence,

00:24:56.740 --> 00:24:59.490
sensation, self-recognition.

00:24:59.490 --> 00:25:02.430
A person bears their own capacities,

00:25:02.430 --> 00:25:06.050
and most importantly to
our discussion today,

00:25:06.050 --> 00:25:10.030
a person is capable of
entering into relationship,

00:25:10.030 --> 00:25:11.830
particularly into

00:25:12.990 --> 00:25:15.463
reciprocal relationships.

00:25:16.960 --> 00:25:19.170
Not only in Potawatomi culture

00:25:19.170 --> 00:25:22.700
and those of many of my relative nations

00:25:22.700 --> 00:25:25.160
are plants viewed as persons,

00:25:25.160 --> 00:25:27.430
but they are viewed as teachers,

00:25:27.430 --> 00:25:31.320
as among our highest,
most revered of teachers,

00:25:31.320 --> 00:25:33.400
and why should this be?

00:25:33.400 --> 00:25:35.560
In the Western world, these organisms,

00:25:35.560 --> 00:25:39.440
which are viewed as practically
nothing, close to the rocks,

00:25:39.440 --> 00:25:43.583
are revered as teachers
among Indigenous peoples.

00:25:44.450 --> 00:25:47.090
Part of it is the understanding
that they have been part,

00:25:47.090 --> 00:25:48.200
they have been here on earth

00:25:48.200 --> 00:25:50.470
far longer than any other beings,

00:25:50.470 --> 00:25:52.550
but there's really the recognition

00:25:52.550 --> 00:25:54.620
that what other beings on earth

00:25:54.620 --> 00:25:59.620
can take light and air
and water, and make food,

00:25:59.620 --> 00:26:00.743
and then give it away?

00:26:02.410 --> 00:26:04.900
Great healthcare plan or food plan.

00:26:04.900 --> 00:26:07.070
They make medicine too.
That's their healthcare plan.

00:26:07.070 --> 00:26:10.350
They make medicines and
give them away for free.

00:26:10.350 --> 00:26:13.410
Plants are understood as generous agents,

00:26:13.410 --> 00:26:15.750
generative agents as well,

00:26:15.750 --> 00:26:19.520
creative beings that teach
us how to be in the world,

00:26:19.520 --> 00:26:20.970
teach us how to,

00:26:20.970 --> 00:26:24.840
what it is to be a
right being in the world

00:26:24.840 --> 00:26:29.400
because you're constantly
engaged in give and take,

00:26:29.400 --> 00:26:33.250
and so you see that if you
come with that worldview

00:26:33.250 --> 00:26:34.850
into the botany lab,

00:26:34.850 --> 00:26:36.520
where you're just simply dissecting plants

00:26:36.520 --> 00:26:38.320
and thinking of them as objects,

00:26:38.320 --> 00:26:40.700
that's kind of an uphill slog,

00:26:40.700 --> 00:26:45.200
so I'm so excited by the new
fields of plant neurobiology

00:26:46.120 --> 00:26:48.500
that are doing some really amazing work

00:26:48.500 --> 00:26:50.350
to demonstrate, in fact,

00:26:50.350 --> 00:26:53.400
that plants do have
their own agency, right?

00:26:53.400 --> 00:26:55.960
That plants can make choices,

00:26:55.960 --> 00:27:00.690
that plants can hear
and respond to sounds,

00:27:00.690 --> 00:27:02.850
that plants have memory,

00:27:02.850 --> 00:27:05.860
that plants can plan ahead.

00:27:05.860 --> 00:27:09.623
All of these attributes of
personhood, of beingness,

00:27:10.520 --> 00:27:14.420
that we're now finding in the
plant biology laboratories

00:27:14.420 --> 00:27:15.850
are, in a sense, an echo

00:27:15.850 --> 00:27:20.380
of what our traditional
teachers always told us,

00:27:20.380 --> 00:27:22.440
and there's certainly no question

00:27:22.440 --> 00:27:24.530
that plants provide generously,

00:27:24.530 --> 00:27:26.870
as their original instructions were to do,

00:27:26.870 --> 00:27:29.330
for all of us, for human beings,

00:27:29.330 --> 00:27:32.840
given all the medicines that they make us,

00:27:32.840 --> 00:27:36.790
the other resources that they make us,

00:27:36.790 --> 00:27:40.440
and I wanna pause for a
moment on that term resources

00:27:40.440 --> 00:27:44.910
because whether it be food or
timber or ecosystem services,

00:27:44.910 --> 00:27:46.760
all of these things are things

00:27:46.760 --> 00:27:50.040
that my natural resource
conservation biology colleagues

00:27:50.040 --> 00:27:54.913
refer to as either ecosystem
services or natural resources,

00:27:55.780 --> 00:27:59.463
and that language has certain
consequences, doesn't it?

00:28:00.350 --> 00:28:04.200
When we think about the
beingness of plants,

00:28:04.200 --> 00:28:05.370
natural resources,

00:28:05.370 --> 00:28:06.740
that which is not valuable

00:28:06.740 --> 00:28:10.003
until it is transmuted into
something that humans need.

00:28:11.220 --> 00:28:13.780
The language in our Potawatomi language,

00:28:13.780 --> 00:28:16.110
of which I am just a beginning student,

00:28:16.110 --> 00:28:21.110
the name that we would give
to natural resources is gifts,

00:28:21.330 --> 00:28:23.420
that all of these are gifts,

00:28:23.420 --> 00:28:28.420
and gifts, therefore, open us
to a relationship, don't they?

00:28:28.430 --> 00:28:31.213
When somebody gives us a gift,

00:28:34.310 --> 00:28:36.560
what do we do with that gift?

00:28:36.560 --> 00:28:38.623
What do we do in response to that gift?

00:28:40.870 --> 00:28:41.740
We what?

00:28:41.740 --> 00:28:42.573
(audience member speaks faintly)

00:28:42.573 --> 00:28:43.470
We say, "Thank you."

00:28:43.470 --> 00:28:44.980
We enter into gratitude,

00:28:44.980 --> 00:28:48.470
and as I said, gratitude
is our first responsibility

00:28:48.470 --> 00:28:51.760
because gratitude acknowledges
the gifts of the world,

00:28:51.760 --> 00:28:52.840
things that we have never,

00:28:52.840 --> 00:28:54.670
that we have not bought and paid for,

00:28:54.670 --> 00:28:57.670
things that we don't necessarily deserve,

00:28:57.670 --> 00:28:59.930
but they are given to us,

00:28:59.930 --> 00:29:02.100
and when somebody gives you a gift,

00:29:02.100 --> 00:29:03.940
yes, you say, "Thank you,"

00:29:03.940 --> 00:29:05.530
and then what?

00:29:05.530 --> 00:29:06.783
What's the next step?

00:29:07.975 --> 00:29:08.808
- [Audience Member] Give back.

00:29:08.808 --> 00:29:10.930
- You give back, right?

00:29:10.930 --> 00:29:14.700
Gratitude and gift open
the door to reciprocity,

00:29:14.700 --> 00:29:16.060
and this is another reason

00:29:16.060 --> 00:29:18.540
that the plants are
understood as our teachers

00:29:18.540 --> 00:29:20.040
because they teach us,

00:29:20.040 --> 00:29:23.740
from the seed to the fruit
and back to the seed,

00:29:23.740 --> 00:29:26.090
they teach us about reciprocity

00:29:27.494 --> 00:29:29.653
and what is our role?

00:29:31.470 --> 00:29:36.470
So if we think about
plants as our teachers,

00:29:36.530 --> 00:29:37.830
the question then becomes

00:29:37.830 --> 00:29:40.150
how can we be better students?

00:29:40.150 --> 00:29:42.240
What is it that they are teaching us?

00:29:42.240 --> 00:29:44.373
And how can we be better students?

00:29:45.890 --> 00:29:48.820
And I wanna tell you
another story about that

00:29:48.820 --> 00:29:51.543
related to notions of sustainability.

00:29:52.640 --> 00:29:56.850
My friend Carol Crowe, who
is an Algonquin biologist,

00:29:56.850 --> 00:29:59.610
went to her band council,
which is the tribal government,

00:29:59.610 --> 00:30:02.880
to ask for a grant to go to a
conference on sustainability,

00:30:02.880 --> 00:30:06.020
and so she went before
her Elders and was asking,

00:30:06.020 --> 00:30:08.360
and they said, "Well,
what's sustainability?"

00:30:08.360 --> 00:30:09.540
And she kind of laughed,

00:30:09.540 --> 00:30:10.847
thinking, "Well, that's the way our people

00:30:10.847 --> 00:30:14.650
"have been living so well in
this place for millennia,"

00:30:14.650 --> 00:30:16.350
but she used these definitions

00:30:16.350 --> 00:30:18.780
that are familiar to all of you, right?

00:30:18.780 --> 00:30:20.690
Lots and lots of different definitions

00:30:20.690 --> 00:30:23.610
of this slippery word, sustainability,

00:30:23.610 --> 00:30:26.890
but in these excerpts that
I've highlighted here,

00:30:26.890 --> 00:30:30.770
one of the things that is
certainly present in all of these,

00:30:30.770 --> 00:30:34.790
ensure the attainment and
continued satisfaction

00:30:34.790 --> 00:30:35.760
of human needs.

00:30:35.760 --> 00:30:37.763
Continue to provide.

00:30:38.660 --> 00:30:42.910
Do not diminish the
potential to meet the needs,

00:30:42.910 --> 00:30:45.200
so she told her Elders all that,

00:30:45.200 --> 00:30:47.073
and they were very quiet,

00:30:48.980 --> 00:30:51.520
and she thought they
were gonna turn her down,

00:30:51.520 --> 00:30:53.687
and they said, "No, we want you to go.

00:30:53.687 --> 00:30:57.797
"It's very important that
you go to that meeting,

00:30:57.797 --> 00:31:00.257
"but we want you to go
and tell them something.

00:31:00.257 --> 00:31:01.397
"Carry a message

00:31:01.397 --> 00:31:05.200
"and tell them that their
idea of sustainability

00:31:06.407 --> 00:31:11.407
"is really a way to just keep on taking."

00:31:11.500 --> 00:31:12.923
Just keep on taking.

00:31:14.060 --> 00:31:15.477
They said to her, "You go,

00:31:15.477 --> 00:31:19.630
"and tell them that it's not
our right to keep taking."

00:31:19.630 --> 00:31:21.620
When our feet hit the
ground in the morning,

00:31:21.620 --> 00:31:26.110
we should be thinking what
it is that we have to give.

00:31:26.110 --> 00:31:26.990
Just as the plants

00:31:26.990 --> 00:31:30.000
are constantly giving
to us in reciprocity,

00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:33.300
what is it that we have to give back

00:31:33.300 --> 00:31:36.100
in return for this shower of gifts

00:31:36.100 --> 00:31:37.823
that comes to us every day?

00:31:39.090 --> 00:31:43.570
And sustainability, as a
powerful cultural goal,

00:31:43.570 --> 00:31:46.203
is completely reframed
by this notion, isn't it?

00:31:47.380 --> 00:31:50.270
Of not about taking, but about giving,

00:31:50.270 --> 00:31:52.350
and this is one of the instances

00:31:52.350 --> 00:31:54.117
in which the lens of Indigenous knowledge

00:31:54.117 --> 00:31:56.180
and environmental philosophy

00:31:56.180 --> 00:31:59.510
can reshape the way that
we think and contribute

00:31:59.510 --> 00:32:03.250
to that cultural evolution
we spoke of at the outset

00:32:03.250 --> 00:32:06.000
because traditional ecological knowledge

00:32:06.000 --> 00:32:10.350
is full of stories and
practices and philosophies

00:32:10.350 --> 00:32:12.710
about ways in which human people

00:32:12.710 --> 00:32:16.430
have a beneficial
relationship with the earth.

00:32:16.430 --> 00:32:18.140
When I teach general ecology,

00:32:18.140 --> 00:32:21.447
I ask my students, "How
well versed are you

00:32:21.447 --> 00:32:24.570
"in negative relationships
between people and the earth?"

00:32:24.570 --> 00:32:25.810
And every hand goes up.

00:32:25.810 --> 00:32:27.820
They know all about that,

00:32:27.820 --> 00:32:30.157
but then, when I ask them the opposite,

00:32:30.157 --> 00:32:32.077
"What about positive relationships

00:32:32.077 --> 00:32:33.390
"between people and the earth?"

00:32:33.390 --> 00:32:34.840
there are very few hands,

00:32:34.840 --> 00:32:36.130
and so how is it

00:32:36.130 --> 00:32:38.090
that we're trying to
train the next generation

00:32:38.090 --> 00:32:39.350
of environmental stewards

00:32:39.350 --> 00:32:41.510
when we cannot even imagine

00:32:41.510 --> 00:32:44.880
what positive, mutualistic,
reciprocal relationships

00:32:44.880 --> 00:32:47.290
might look like with the earth,

00:32:47.290 --> 00:32:51.670
and this lens, this framework
from the Indigenous worldview,

00:32:51.670 --> 00:32:54.410
is a powerful tool for moving there.

00:32:54.410 --> 00:32:57.950
In fact, what Carol's Elders,

00:32:57.950 --> 00:33:00.450
the Cree Elders were essentially saying

00:33:00.450 --> 00:33:02.430
is that we should be thinking

00:33:02.430 --> 00:33:05.310
not what more can we take from the earth?

00:33:05.310 --> 00:33:07.740
But what does the earth ask of us?

00:33:07.740 --> 00:33:09.960
What is our role as human people

00:33:11.184 --> 00:33:13.150
in response to these gifts?

00:33:13.150 --> 00:33:17.010
How do we reciprocate
the many gifts of plants

00:33:17.010 --> 00:33:20.400
from every breath of oxygen that we take,

00:33:20.400 --> 00:33:24.670
to food, to medicine, to
beauty, to companionship,

00:33:24.670 --> 00:33:26.923
to them being our teachers?

00:33:28.060 --> 00:33:30.950
And one of the most powerful ways

00:33:30.950 --> 00:33:34.580
in a world that is full of
Monarch butterflies and goldenrod

00:33:34.580 --> 00:33:38.550
and asters and redwoods and
salamanders and bluejays,

00:33:38.550 --> 00:33:41.810
a world which is full of
all of these other beings,

00:33:41.810 --> 00:33:45.023
shouldn't we at least be paying attention?

00:33:47.160 --> 00:33:47.993
And we're not.

00:33:49.530 --> 00:33:51.440
Our whole society suffers greatly

00:33:51.440 --> 00:33:54.300
from what has been called plant blindness,

00:33:54.300 --> 00:33:58.800
of not really even being
aware of the gifts of plants,

00:33:58.800 --> 00:34:03.150
and one of the ways that we
talk about paying attention

00:34:03.150 --> 00:34:04.763
is by knowing names,

00:34:05.870 --> 00:34:07.020
and yeah, I'm a botanist,

00:34:07.020 --> 00:34:08.387
so, of course, you think, "Well, yep,

00:34:08.387 --> 00:34:10.820
"she's gonna go on about
plant taxonomy now."

00:34:10.820 --> 00:34:13.150
Well, yeah, a little, (chuckles)

00:34:13.150 --> 00:34:14.930
but more than that,

00:34:14.930 --> 00:34:17.720
when you come to know the
name of your neighbor,

00:34:17.720 --> 00:34:20.290
doesn't that bring you into relationship?

00:34:20.290 --> 00:34:23.040
It opens the potential for reciprocity.

00:34:23.040 --> 00:34:24.430
When you don't even bother

00:34:24.430 --> 00:34:26.290
to learn the name of somebody around you,

00:34:26.290 --> 00:34:28.030
that's a sign of disrespect,

00:34:28.030 --> 00:34:30.730
and so by not knowing the name

00:34:30.730 --> 00:34:34.340
of the one who makes the
aspirin for you every day

00:34:34.340 --> 00:34:36.040
or the one who makes the syrup

00:34:36.040 --> 00:34:38.310
that you pour on your
pancakes on Sunday morning,

00:34:38.310 --> 00:34:39.890
if you don't even know their names,

00:34:39.890 --> 00:34:41.630
it's an act of disrespect,

00:34:41.630 --> 00:34:44.530
and so there's the
notion of learning names

00:34:44.530 --> 00:34:46.413
by paying attention.

00:34:47.310 --> 00:34:48.410
This little plant

00:34:48.410 --> 00:34:51.490
grows in everybody's
backyard here, I'm sure,

00:34:51.490 --> 00:34:53.380
or along your sidewalk.

00:34:53.380 --> 00:34:55.267
Does anybody know this plant?

00:34:55.267 --> 00:34:56.100
- [Audience Member] Heal-all.

00:34:56.100 --> 00:34:56.990
(audience member speaks indistinctly)

00:34:56.990 --> 00:34:58.330
- Yes. Wonderful.

00:34:58.330 --> 00:35:01.160
Say that again. I'm in a
botanically literate audience.

00:35:01.160 --> 00:35:04.140
Heal-all. That would be a good
name to know, wouldn't it?

00:35:04.140 --> 00:35:06.450
If you just needed to
start with one plant name,

00:35:06.450 --> 00:35:09.850
heal-all would probably be a good one.

00:35:09.850 --> 00:35:11.360
Remarkable plant.

00:35:11.360 --> 00:35:14.130
I wish I had time to talk
all about its chemistry,

00:35:14.130 --> 00:35:14.963
but it does.

00:35:14.963 --> 00:35:19.424
It really heals a lot.
- What was that (indistinct)?

00:35:19.424 --> 00:35:21.260
What was the last one?

00:35:21.260 --> 00:35:22.170
- The plants?

00:35:22.170 --> 00:35:23.003
- [Audience Member] Yes.

00:35:23.003 --> 00:35:26.400
- This is a camas
meadow, Camassia quamash,

00:35:27.690 --> 00:35:30.760
a plant, again, all of
these plants are in here

00:35:30.760 --> 00:35:32.930
because they are stories of reciprocity.

00:35:32.930 --> 00:35:35.790
This is a plant that people
need to tend by burning

00:35:35.790 --> 00:35:39.333
in order to bring the camas to fruit.

00:35:41.745 --> 00:35:43.662
Okay, paying attention,

00:35:46.110 --> 00:35:48.250
knowing names,

00:35:48.250 --> 00:35:50.050
a number of educators, of course,

00:35:50.050 --> 00:35:53.360
have pointed out that we
are natural taxonomists.

00:35:53.360 --> 00:35:55.880
We categorize, we learn names like crazy,

00:35:55.880 --> 00:35:59.010
and our kids, and let's
face it, ourselves,

00:35:59.010 --> 00:36:00.610
we're pretty good at this, aren't we?

00:36:00.610 --> 00:36:02.800
Recognizing corporate logos.

00:36:02.800 --> 00:36:05.440
Who is it that really
sustains us in the world?

00:36:05.440 --> 00:36:08.130
Where are we giving our attention?

00:36:08.130 --> 00:36:11.150
Our natural means of, or natural tendency

00:36:11.150 --> 00:36:13.730
to pay attention to that
which supports our life

00:36:13.730 --> 00:36:17.620
has, in a sense, been
hijacked by advertisers.

00:36:17.620 --> 00:36:19.690
It is, to me, terrifying

00:36:19.690 --> 00:36:23.050
that kids can recognize
100 corporate logos

00:36:23.050 --> 00:36:26.330
and recognize fewer than 10 plants.

00:36:26.330 --> 00:36:29.540
The average American knows
fewer than 10 plants,

00:36:29.540 --> 00:36:31.560
and you know what tops the list?

00:36:31.560 --> 00:36:34.680
Christmas tree and grass,

00:36:34.680 --> 00:36:37.760
so those don't hardly even count, right?

00:36:37.760 --> 00:36:39.730
So we have a problem with plant blindness.

00:36:39.730 --> 00:36:43.170
How can we understand and
relate to the world as gift

00:36:43.170 --> 00:36:45.520
if we don't even know the gift-givers,

00:36:45.520 --> 00:36:47.290
if we don't even know their names

00:36:47.290 --> 00:36:50.350
or anything about what
it is that they offer us

00:36:50.350 --> 00:36:51.650
every single day?

00:36:51.650 --> 00:36:55.360
How can we be better students
of plants? Pay attention.

00:36:55.360 --> 00:36:56.670
Know about their gifts.

00:36:56.670 --> 00:37:00.150
Share that knowledge
with those around you,

00:37:00.150 --> 00:37:02.160
and speaking of plant names,

00:37:02.160 --> 00:37:04.620
I said I'd get back to Linnaeus, right?

00:37:04.620 --> 00:37:08.463
Hierochloe odorata that he
gave the name to sweetgrass.

00:37:09.372 --> 00:37:13.390
What you see here are two
great botanists and namers.

00:37:13.390 --> 00:37:16.210
We all know that Linnaeus
is the great namer,

00:37:16.210 --> 00:37:18.290
and when we all took Bio 101,

00:37:18.290 --> 00:37:19.910
we were told that he was, in fact,

00:37:19.910 --> 00:37:22.970
the father of naming and taxonomy

00:37:23.920 --> 00:37:28.920
because before 1770, no one
had names for plants? (laughs)

00:37:29.870 --> 00:37:31.490
Well, on the other side,

00:37:31.490 --> 00:37:35.530
you see Nanabush,
Nanabozho in our culture.

00:37:35.530 --> 00:37:37.850
You know, when Nanabozho came to the earth

00:37:37.850 --> 00:37:40.560
as the first man, Anishinaabe man,

00:37:40.560 --> 00:37:43.090
he was given a job by the creator,

00:37:43.090 --> 00:37:46.223
and his job was to give names,

00:37:48.280 --> 00:37:51.930
but how was he different from Linnaeus?

00:37:51.930 --> 00:37:55.540
Linnaeus bestowed names in
a foreign language, right?

00:37:55.540 --> 00:37:58.593
In a standardized scientific nomenclature,

00:37:59.520 --> 00:38:01.450
he bestowed names,

00:38:01.450 --> 00:38:02.480
and naming, as we know,

00:38:02.480 --> 00:38:06.883
is a powerful act of control,
as well as attention.

00:38:07.840 --> 00:38:09.320
How did Nanabush do it?

00:38:09.320 --> 00:38:12.090
Nanabush didn't give names.

00:38:12.090 --> 00:38:14.820
His job was to wander the world

00:38:14.820 --> 00:38:18.420
getting to know all the
plants and animals so well

00:38:18.420 --> 00:38:21.423
that they would reveal
to him their real names,

00:38:22.360 --> 00:38:26.210
and here we see, again,
this subject versus object.

00:38:26.210 --> 00:38:28.780
Who has the right to give a name?

00:38:28.780 --> 00:38:31.520
If it's an object, you can
call it anything you want.

00:38:31.520 --> 00:38:33.920
If it's a subject, you wouldn't presume.

00:38:33.920 --> 00:38:38.920
You would wait to know and
have the true name be revealed,

00:38:38.920 --> 00:38:41.100
so we have, again, this different lenses

00:38:41.100 --> 00:38:44.350
for looking at the beingness of nature

00:38:44.350 --> 00:38:47.210
as object and subject,

00:38:47.210 --> 00:38:49.150
and this brings me to...

00:38:49.150 --> 00:38:49.983
How am I doing on...

00:38:49.983 --> 00:38:52.120
There we go. I can see the clock now.

00:38:52.120 --> 00:38:54.570
A peculiarity of language.

00:38:54.570 --> 00:38:57.290
We talked about natural
resources versus gift

00:38:57.290 --> 00:39:00.010
as an interesting linguistic idea,

00:39:00.010 --> 00:39:02.360
but I'd like you to think
for just a little bit

00:39:03.260 --> 00:39:07.360
about how we refer to the living world

00:39:07.360 --> 00:39:08.880
in the English language.

00:39:08.880 --> 00:39:11.490
All these beings, all these gifts,

00:39:11.490 --> 00:39:14.460
and if I was to say something

00:39:14.460 --> 00:39:16.840
about (speaks in Potawatomi), the bear,

00:39:16.840 --> 00:39:21.230
I would have to say it's
scratching the tree, right?

00:39:21.230 --> 00:39:22.063
It.

00:39:22.063 --> 00:39:26.080
I would have to refer to
all of these beings as it

00:39:26.080 --> 00:39:29.720
because in English, we have
a very peculiar grammar

00:39:29.720 --> 00:39:32.470
that we don't often think about,

00:39:32.470 --> 00:39:36.083
but in English, you're either
a human or you're an it.

00:39:38.290 --> 00:39:40.500
If your grandma was standing there

00:39:41.690 --> 00:39:44.140
making soup at the stove,

00:39:44.140 --> 00:39:47.597
and someone said, "Oh, look,
it's wearing an apron."

00:39:50.280 --> 00:39:51.570
That's not right.

00:39:51.570 --> 00:39:52.800
It's not only rude,

00:39:52.800 --> 00:39:55.760
but we just really
disrespected her, right?

00:39:55.760 --> 00:39:59.153
We took her humanity, her
personhood away from her.

00:40:00.130 --> 00:40:03.560
She who was about to provide for us,

00:40:03.560 --> 00:40:05.380
but in English, how do we speak

00:40:05.380 --> 00:40:08.970
of all of those bearers of
gifts in the natural world

00:40:08.970 --> 00:40:11.260
who provide for us?

00:40:11.260 --> 00:40:14.170
We only say it of them, right?

00:40:14.170 --> 00:40:18.560
And even in our language, in
our beautiful English language,

00:40:18.560 --> 00:40:21.880
this notion of object and
the lack of personhood,

00:40:21.880 --> 00:40:24.600
lack of beingness is present,

00:40:24.600 --> 00:40:27.340
and I think that we can think about this

00:40:27.340 --> 00:40:29.307
in the consequences for our worldview

00:40:29.307 --> 00:40:32.370
and the way that we relate to the land.

00:40:32.370 --> 00:40:35.450
This imposition of the notion of it

00:40:35.450 --> 00:40:38.620
is really a tool of
linguistic imperialism.

00:40:38.620 --> 00:40:40.830
It is a tool of colonization,

00:40:40.830 --> 00:40:43.040
which we feel acutely today

00:40:43.040 --> 00:40:45.423
in our relationships
with the living world,

00:40:46.400 --> 00:40:50.070
and, you know, in the Potawatomi language

00:40:50.070 --> 00:40:53.913
it's impossible to say
it of the living world.

00:40:55.340 --> 00:40:56.720
You can't do it.

00:40:56.720 --> 00:40:59.700
We speak of that bluejay
and of that strawberry

00:40:59.700 --> 00:41:03.780
with the same grammar that
we use for our family members

00:41:03.780 --> 00:41:05.593
because they are our family members,

00:41:06.650 --> 00:41:10.640
and so encoded in this
little piece of grammar

00:41:10.640 --> 00:41:12.010
is a worldview, isn't it?

00:41:12.010 --> 00:41:13.960
Of objectified nature,

00:41:13.960 --> 00:41:15.890
and if nature is an object,

00:41:15.890 --> 00:41:19.530
if it's not made up of beings and persons,

00:41:19.530 --> 00:41:23.410
then we have a certain kind
of permission, if you will,

00:41:23.410 --> 00:41:24.870
to exploit it

00:41:24.870 --> 00:41:28.830
because when we objectify people,

00:41:28.830 --> 00:41:31.060
when we objectify nature,

00:41:31.060 --> 00:41:32.850
we're very good at this,

00:41:32.850 --> 00:41:35.400
it creates a circle of moral exclusion.

00:41:35.400 --> 00:41:36.800
It says you're other,

00:41:36.800 --> 00:41:38.220
and generally speaking,

00:41:38.220 --> 00:41:40.530
where are natural beings
on that hierarchy?

00:41:40.530 --> 00:41:44.370
Those others are lower-than,
lesser-than beings,

00:41:44.370 --> 00:41:48.160
and so this objectification of nature,

00:41:48.160 --> 00:41:52.190
which is captured in our
worldview and in our language,

00:41:52.190 --> 00:41:56.440
I think is emblematic of our relationship,

00:41:56.440 --> 00:41:58.030
and so what I'd like to do

00:41:58.030 --> 00:42:01.643
is to invite you into a little experiment,

00:42:02.600 --> 00:42:05.810
to think about how Indigenous
languages the world over

00:42:05.810 --> 00:42:08.310
have this concept of
animacy in the language,

00:42:08.310 --> 00:42:11.950
that you cannot say it
about a living being.

00:42:11.950 --> 00:42:13.530
It's just not possible.

00:42:13.530 --> 00:42:16.470
We speak a grammar of animacy,

00:42:16.470 --> 00:42:20.600
and could we, in some
way, animate English?

00:42:20.600 --> 00:42:24.730
Could we step away from
objectification of the living world

00:42:24.730 --> 00:42:29.020
by being very conscious
about the way that we speak?

00:42:29.020 --> 00:42:31.770
And so the proposal

00:42:32.930 --> 00:42:34.760
is a modest proposal.

00:42:34.760 --> 00:42:37.610
The pronouns of the revolution.

00:42:37.610 --> 00:42:39.780
Reclaim the grammar of animacy.

00:42:39.780 --> 00:42:42.910
Change, begin to change our thinking

00:42:42.910 --> 00:42:44.310
and the way that we behave

00:42:44.310 --> 00:42:46.900
in the living world through grammar,

00:42:46.900 --> 00:42:48.500
and I thought for a long time of

00:42:48.500 --> 00:42:50.720
how in the world could you do this?

00:42:50.720 --> 00:42:53.587
So I asked one of my Elders,
my language teachers,

00:42:53.587 --> 00:42:55.677
"Is there a word in Potawatomi

00:42:55.677 --> 00:42:58.070
"for a living being of the earth?"

00:42:58.070 --> 00:43:00.830
so that they don't, not to
say that they are humans.

00:43:00.830 --> 00:43:04.210
It's not anthropomorphism,
but you're a being.

00:43:04.210 --> 00:43:05.340
Is there a word?

00:43:05.340 --> 00:43:08.150
And there is; it's bmaadiziaki,

00:43:08.150 --> 00:43:12.080
and it simply means a living
being of the good earth,

00:43:12.080 --> 00:43:14.300
actually means a good
being of the living earth.

00:43:14.300 --> 00:43:17.840
That's right, and so
there is a word for it.

00:43:17.840 --> 00:43:22.807
There is a concept for it, which
is built into our language,

00:43:22.807 --> 00:43:26.523
and when we look at that
beautiful word, bmaadiziaki,

00:43:28.206 --> 00:43:32.090
in a culture where we can,
where we rarely tolerate

00:43:32.090 --> 00:43:34.800
speaking a language other than English,

00:43:34.800 --> 00:43:37.120
I don't think that incorporation

00:43:37.120 --> 00:43:41.950
of bmaadiziaki into our language
is really going to work,

00:43:41.950 --> 00:43:45.070
but what about ki?

00:43:45.070 --> 00:43:46.400
Ki, that last phoneme

00:43:46.400 --> 00:43:50.250
on the beautiful word aki,
which means the earth,

00:43:50.250 --> 00:43:54.443
which sounds a bit like he and she.

00:43:56.110 --> 00:44:00.250
Could we refer to living
beings, not as its,

00:44:00.250 --> 00:44:03.600
with a disrespectful objectification

00:44:03.600 --> 00:44:06.880
as if they were just a
paperclip or a pencil.

00:44:06.880 --> 00:44:09.940
We use it for them, not
for sacred living beings

00:44:09.940 --> 00:44:13.100
who are our teachers and our providers.

00:44:13.100 --> 00:44:15.897
Could we say of that
maple tree in the spring,

00:44:15.897 --> 00:44:18.357
"Ki is giving us sap this year?

00:44:18.357 --> 00:44:22.050
"Ki's branches are waving in the wind."

00:44:22.050 --> 00:44:24.410
Could we refine our language

00:44:24.410 --> 00:44:26.810
and pause for a moment to think:

00:44:26.810 --> 00:44:30.113
are these living beings
or are they objects?

00:44:31.170 --> 00:44:33.810
And I went back and forth with my Elders

00:44:33.810 --> 00:44:36.010
talking about this word,

00:44:36.010 --> 00:44:37.610
and we had some concerns

00:44:39.650 --> 00:44:43.490
about cultural appropriation
associated with this,

00:44:43.490 --> 00:44:46.280
with inspiring a new grammar of animacy,

00:44:46.280 --> 00:44:50.210
and we had lots of very
interesting discussions about that,

00:44:50.210 --> 00:44:51.760
so we were really grateful

00:44:51.760 --> 00:44:56.180
that English already has a word

00:44:56.180 --> 00:44:58.770
that works for the plural of ki

00:45:00.060 --> 00:45:05.060
because in Potawatomi,
to make a word plural,

00:45:05.160 --> 00:45:06.383
you add an N.

00:45:09.450 --> 00:45:10.750
We already have that word.

00:45:11.710 --> 00:45:13.340
We already have that word

00:45:13.340 --> 00:45:17.190
so that when the geese
fly back in the spring,

00:45:17.190 --> 00:45:19.677
we can say, "Kin are filling the skies.

00:45:19.677 --> 00:45:21.610
"Welcome back,"

00:45:21.610 --> 00:45:24.010
so that every time we
speak of the living world,

00:45:24.010 --> 00:45:25.620
we speak of relationship,

00:45:25.620 --> 00:45:29.703
of respectful relationship
with place and with beings.

00:45:31.510 --> 00:45:34.623
How do we reciprocate
the gifts of the plants?

00:45:37.280 --> 00:45:39.220
This notion of personhood

00:45:39.220 --> 00:45:43.610
is far more than a notion of a pronoun.

00:45:43.610 --> 00:45:44.880
What we're really talking about

00:45:44.880 --> 00:45:47.520
is extension of thinking about personhood

00:45:47.520 --> 00:45:49.640
to living beings, right?

00:45:49.640 --> 00:45:53.743
And this has ramifications
in the political,

00:45:55.280 --> 00:45:58.160
legal worlds,

00:45:58.160 --> 00:45:59.870
as well as the ecological worlds

00:45:59.870 --> 00:46:01.810
because what you're looking at here

00:46:01.810 --> 00:46:04.620
is the sacred river of the Maori people.

00:46:04.620 --> 00:46:06.790
This is the Whanganui River

00:46:06.790 --> 00:46:09.640
that four years ago

00:46:09.640 --> 00:46:11.880
was declared a legal person

00:46:13.640 --> 00:46:17.240
out of the Maori concepts
of animacy of all beings.

00:46:17.240 --> 00:46:21.900
That river is understood
and has legal personhood,

00:46:21.900 --> 00:46:24.720
so decisions about that
river need to be made

00:46:24.720 --> 00:46:28.830
for the wellbeing of that river,

00:46:28.830 --> 00:46:30.810
so these ideas of personhood

00:46:31.760 --> 00:46:33.600
are not kind of arcane constructs;

00:46:33.600 --> 00:46:36.780
they have real meaning in the world.

00:46:36.780 --> 00:46:38.170
It's really interesting, isn't it?

00:46:38.170 --> 00:46:40.010
That in this country,

00:46:40.010 --> 00:46:44.800
the only things that can be
recognized as legal persons,

00:46:44.800 --> 00:46:49.670
aside from ourselves,
are corporations, right?

00:46:49.670 --> 00:46:50.870
But the rest of the,

00:46:50.870 --> 00:46:53.040
particularly in the Indigenous-led world

00:46:53.040 --> 00:46:55.210
has other ideas about that

00:46:55.210 --> 00:46:57.220
because, as you know, in Ecuador,

00:46:57.220 --> 00:46:59.410
the constitution of Ecuador

00:46:59.410 --> 00:47:02.230
enshrines the personhood of nature,

00:47:02.230 --> 00:47:04.310
assigning rights to Pachamama,

00:47:04.310 --> 00:47:07.760
rights that Mother Nature has

00:47:07.760 --> 00:47:10.320
her own inherent rights.

00:47:10.320 --> 00:47:14.550
Implementation of this
is far more difficult

00:47:14.550 --> 00:47:16.470
than conceptualization of it,

00:47:16.470 --> 00:47:17.530
but here we have it,

00:47:17.530 --> 00:47:20.590
and it's true in Bolivia as well,

00:47:20.590 --> 00:47:23.210
and Bolivia was the leader of a coalition

00:47:23.210 --> 00:47:25.080
to bring before the United Nations

00:47:25.080 --> 00:47:28.020
the Universal Declaration of
the Rights of Mother Earth,

00:47:28.020 --> 00:47:30.690
which is also this notion of personhood,

00:47:30.690 --> 00:47:34.080
of subjecthood for Mother Nature,

00:47:34.080 --> 00:47:35.780
for Pachamama, for Gaia,

00:47:35.780 --> 00:47:38.800
for all the different languages
in which we think of her,

00:47:38.800 --> 00:47:43.130
and so these ideas from
Indigenous ways of thinking,

00:47:43.130 --> 00:47:47.070
of plants as persons, as
teachers, as esteemed beings,

00:47:47.070 --> 00:47:49.900
is finding its way into international law.

00:47:49.900 --> 00:47:52.950
The Paris climate talks
was one of the first

00:47:55.320 --> 00:47:58.410
courts for crimes against nature

00:47:59.350 --> 00:48:02.623
in addition, parallel to
crimes against humanity,

00:48:04.350 --> 00:48:09.220
so if we consider plants to be persons,

00:48:09.220 --> 00:48:10.540
that has implications

00:48:10.540 --> 00:48:14.233
for how we relate to plants
as consumers, does it not?

00:48:15.400 --> 00:48:17.940
If plants are persons,

00:48:17.940 --> 00:48:19.160
in the Western world,

00:48:19.160 --> 00:48:21.440
where we say that plants are not persons

00:48:21.440 --> 00:48:24.020
or even so much, in many cases, beings,

00:48:24.020 --> 00:48:25.480
certainly not legal persons,

00:48:25.480 --> 00:48:27.433
we refer to them all as it,

00:48:28.290 --> 00:48:30.650
that permission to exploit

00:48:30.650 --> 00:48:32.420
means that we don't really have to worry

00:48:32.420 --> 00:48:34.370
about the ethical consequences of it

00:48:34.370 --> 00:48:35.830
'cause it's just a thing,

00:48:35.830 --> 00:48:38.140
and they are outside our circle

00:48:38.140 --> 00:48:42.850
of moral or ecological compassion.

00:48:42.850 --> 00:48:45.110
In Indigenous ways of thinking,

00:48:45.110 --> 00:48:46.830
remember, if we're
thinking about the plants

00:48:46.830 --> 00:48:49.470
as beings, as teachers, as our relatives,

00:48:49.470 --> 00:48:50.720
and we're gonna eat them?

00:48:51.640 --> 00:48:55.253
This sets up certain
ethical tensions, for sure,

00:48:56.290 --> 00:48:58.900
but it's really important
that they're there

00:48:58.900 --> 00:49:01.180
because as (indistinct), as human beings,

00:49:01.180 --> 00:49:02.570
we can't photosynthesize.

00:49:02.570 --> 00:49:04.310
We have to eat plants.

00:49:04.310 --> 00:49:05.770
We can't just call them it

00:49:05.770 --> 00:49:08.530
and have no ethics associated with it.

00:49:08.530 --> 00:49:11.160
We recognize them as
persons and our relatives,

00:49:11.160 --> 00:49:13.650
so we have to have very special ethics

00:49:13.650 --> 00:49:17.130
associated with honoring
the lives that they give us,

00:49:17.130 --> 00:49:21.110
and this is encoded in
a series of practices

00:49:21.110 --> 00:49:25.310
which are understood as
the Honorable Harvest,

00:49:25.310 --> 00:49:27.130
so when we recognize the plants

00:49:27.130 --> 00:49:30.340
as gift-givers, as beings, as teachers,

00:49:30.340 --> 00:49:32.180
rules come up

00:49:32.180 --> 00:49:36.300
for essentially consuming
your beloved family,

00:49:36.300 --> 00:49:40.660
and these rules were taught
to me as a berry picker,

00:49:40.660 --> 00:49:42.573
as a medicine gatherer,

00:49:43.700 --> 00:49:45.810
and they may seem, at first,

00:49:45.810 --> 00:49:47.660
like kind of quaint prescriptions

00:49:47.660 --> 00:49:50.620
for how it is that we go berry picking,

00:49:50.620 --> 00:49:52.210
but I think you'll see

00:49:52.210 --> 00:49:56.420
that they are potentially very strong ways

00:49:56.420 --> 00:49:57.770
to reconceptualize

00:49:57.770 --> 00:50:00.370
and enact our relationship with the earth.

00:50:00.370 --> 00:50:03.690
When I go to pick berries,
maybe this is same for you,

00:50:03.690 --> 00:50:05.440
we say that we never take the first one.

00:50:05.440 --> 00:50:07.200
We come upon the first ones,

00:50:07.200 --> 00:50:09.830
and we greet them, and
say "Thank you" to them,

00:50:09.830 --> 00:50:12.320
and we pass them by.

00:50:12.320 --> 00:50:13.410
This self-restraint

00:50:13.410 --> 00:50:15.860
is an inherent conservation
ethic, isn't it?

00:50:15.860 --> 00:50:17.850
Because if you don't take the first one,

00:50:17.850 --> 00:50:20.920
you're not going to take the last one,

00:50:20.920 --> 00:50:23.370
and when we come to the
one that we can take,

00:50:23.370 --> 00:50:25.050
in some cultures, it's the third one,

00:50:25.050 --> 00:50:27.570
some cultures, it's the seventh one,

00:50:27.570 --> 00:50:29.210
we ask permission.

00:50:29.210 --> 00:50:32.670
We actually speak to that
plant, introduce ourselves,

00:50:32.670 --> 00:50:35.920
and ask that plant if
it has enough to share.

00:50:35.920 --> 00:50:37.070
Would you allow me

00:50:37.070 --> 00:50:39.170
to bring some medicine
home for my daughter?

00:50:39.170 --> 00:50:41.910
Some berries home for that pie?

00:50:41.910 --> 00:50:44.020
We ask permission,

00:50:44.020 --> 00:50:47.490
and I understand that if you
came upon somebody in the woods

00:50:47.490 --> 00:50:50.000
who was talking to a
patch of strawberries,

00:50:50.000 --> 00:50:53.610
you might think that they
were a little over the edge,

00:50:53.610 --> 00:50:55.620
but in our culture,
it's just good manners.

00:50:55.620 --> 00:50:56.810
It's good manners.

00:50:56.810 --> 00:50:59.210
Would you go into your grandmother's house

00:50:59.210 --> 00:51:01.913
and start rifling through
her cupboards for cookies?

00:51:03.570 --> 00:51:04.830
Yeah, you laughed. Yeah.

00:51:04.830 --> 00:51:09.320
Only once. (laughs) You'd
only do that once, right?

00:51:09.320 --> 00:51:10.360
And in a sense,

00:51:10.360 --> 00:51:12.910
that's how we treat our
beloved grandmother the earth.

00:51:12.910 --> 00:51:14.700
We just go take whatever we want,

00:51:14.700 --> 00:51:16.887
but the Honorable Harvest
says, "No, you don't.

00:51:16.887 --> 00:51:18.540
"You ask permission."

00:51:18.540 --> 00:51:19.910
You ask permission,

00:51:19.910 --> 00:51:22.310
and then your grandma
will probably give you

00:51:22.310 --> 00:51:25.800
all the cookies that you
want on her best china plate,

00:51:25.800 --> 00:51:27.610
and if you ask permission,

00:51:27.610 --> 00:51:30.000
you have to listen for the answer,

00:51:30.000 --> 00:51:33.900
and understanding plants
as beings, as persons,

00:51:33.900 --> 00:51:35.810
there are many ways to answer that.

00:51:35.810 --> 00:51:38.550
As a scientist, I listen for that answer

00:51:38.550 --> 00:51:40.190
and look at the population density

00:51:40.190 --> 00:51:41.790
and look at the vigor of the plants.

00:51:41.790 --> 00:51:44.490
Do they have enough to
share? So on and so forth.

00:51:44.490 --> 00:51:47.390
There are biological, empirical ways

00:51:47.390 --> 00:51:49.230
to listen for that answer,

00:51:49.230 --> 00:51:53.160
but there are also intuitive
ways to listen for that answer,

00:51:53.160 --> 00:51:56.160
and if the answer is no, you go home.

00:51:56.160 --> 00:51:58.180
You go home with an empty basket

00:51:58.180 --> 00:51:59.883
because they don't belong to you.

00:52:01.262 --> 00:52:03.812
If we take without permission,
what is that called?

00:52:04.870 --> 00:52:05.830
Stealing.

00:52:05.830 --> 00:52:07.970
Right, so we take with honor.

00:52:07.970 --> 00:52:11.113
We don't take unless we have permission.

00:52:12.210 --> 00:52:15.603
Take only what you need, of
course. We all know that.

00:52:16.460 --> 00:52:18.013
Much harder to practice.

00:52:19.230 --> 00:52:22.080
Minimize harm in the way
that you're taking it.

00:52:22.080 --> 00:52:25.090
The lesson that was given
to me is never use a shovel

00:52:25.090 --> 00:52:27.210
if a digging stick will do.

00:52:27.210 --> 00:52:28.760
Use appropriate technology.

00:52:28.760 --> 00:52:29.870
Minimize the harm

00:52:29.870 --> 00:52:33.593
that comes from your taking
from the natural world.

00:52:34.860 --> 00:52:37.270
Use everything that you take

00:52:37.270 --> 00:52:40.330
because you are taking a life.

00:52:40.330 --> 00:52:41.560
You are taking a life

00:52:41.560 --> 00:52:43.200
or a life is being given to you

00:52:43.200 --> 00:52:45.400
in this gift from the plants.

00:52:45.400 --> 00:52:47.320
This applies to animals as well,

00:52:47.320 --> 00:52:49.133
and we don't disrespect that.

00:52:50.670 --> 00:52:53.403
Practice gratitude. Be grateful.

00:52:54.260 --> 00:52:57.370
Be grateful for everything
that is given to you,

00:52:57.370 --> 00:52:59.460
and gratitude, again,

00:52:59.460 --> 00:53:04.460
may seem like sort of a hollow
good manners or politeness,

00:53:04.530 --> 00:53:06.840
but gratitude is really powerful

00:53:06.840 --> 00:53:08.590
because when you practice gratitude,

00:53:08.590 --> 00:53:09.660
it helps you remember

00:53:09.660 --> 00:53:12.180
that you have everything
that you need already.

00:53:12.180 --> 00:53:15.100
It makes you feel abundant,

00:53:15.100 --> 00:53:17.700
and gratitude, practicing gratitude

00:53:17.700 --> 00:53:20.790
brings you into relationship
with all that is,

00:53:20.790 --> 00:53:22.760
with those beings.

00:53:22.760 --> 00:53:27.320
You can't feel separated
when you're grateful,

00:53:27.320 --> 00:53:28.640
and you know what?

00:53:28.640 --> 00:53:29.510
I've recently read

00:53:29.510 --> 00:53:32.690
some really interesting
studies about consumption,

00:53:32.690 --> 00:53:35.010
and we know that it is consumption

00:53:35.010 --> 00:53:38.700
that has us hanging on the
brink of climate chaos.

00:53:38.700 --> 00:53:42.493
It is consumption that has led
us into the sixth extinction,

00:53:43.730 --> 00:53:44.563
and you know,

00:53:44.563 --> 00:53:47.350
one of the correlates to
restraint on consumption

00:53:48.380 --> 00:53:50.010
is gratitude.

00:53:50.010 --> 00:53:53.423
People who practice
gratitude consume less,

00:53:54.820 --> 00:53:59.223
so this ethic has real
concrete repercussions.

00:54:00.650 --> 00:54:02.840
Share everything that you've taken.

00:54:02.840 --> 00:54:04.603
The plants have shared with you,

00:54:05.560 --> 00:54:07.730
and so we, then, have to
share what we've taken.

00:54:07.730 --> 00:54:08.900
You take those strawberries

00:54:08.900 --> 00:54:12.823
and give them to somebody else
as well as to your family,

00:54:13.970 --> 00:54:18.390
and always reciprocate the gift.

00:54:18.390 --> 00:54:21.840
In return for those
berries, for that root,

00:54:21.840 --> 00:54:24.170
you need to give something back,

00:54:24.170 --> 00:54:25.310
and there's a lot of teachings

00:54:25.310 --> 00:54:28.133
about what that something could be.

00:54:30.520 --> 00:54:32.270
It could be, in the case of berries,

00:54:32.270 --> 00:54:33.780
you're gonna weed around them

00:54:33.780 --> 00:54:36.130
and reduce the competition for them.

00:54:36.130 --> 00:54:38.420
It could be you're
gonna pick the bugs off.

00:54:38.420 --> 00:54:40.060
It could be you're gonna take the seeds

00:54:40.060 --> 00:54:42.930
and carry them to a new
place and plant them.

00:54:42.930 --> 00:54:44.620
Really practical things

00:54:44.620 --> 00:54:47.680
that your gift of attention and respect

00:54:47.680 --> 00:54:49.250
pay off for that plant

00:54:49.250 --> 00:54:53.203
so that you flourish and the
plant flourishes as well.

00:54:54.100 --> 00:54:58.930
Oftentimes, the gifts are
also spiritual in nature,

00:54:58.930 --> 00:55:02.160
giving a prayer, giving
words, giving a song,

00:55:02.160 --> 00:55:04.620
giving, in my culture,
tobacco, sacred plant,

00:55:04.620 --> 00:55:07.155
giving back to that plant,

00:55:07.155 --> 00:55:08.950
so reciprocating the gift

00:55:08.950 --> 00:55:13.633
is a very important law of taking,

00:55:14.840 --> 00:55:17.150
and I'm almost out of time,

00:55:17.150 --> 00:55:20.960
so I think what I wanna do
is to close with this notion

00:55:20.960 --> 00:55:25.113
of reciprocity and the gift.

00:55:26.760 --> 00:55:30.690
In our culture, we are told
that gifts and responsibilities

00:55:30.690 --> 00:55:34.273
are two sides of the same coin,

00:55:35.290 --> 00:55:39.330
so that when we think about a bird

00:55:39.330 --> 00:55:41.690
singing in the morning,

00:55:41.690 --> 00:55:43.397
we'd say, "Well, that bird was given

00:55:43.397 --> 00:55:45.567
"this beautiful gift of song,"

00:55:46.590 --> 00:55:49.330
and then the whole world
receives it as a gift

00:55:49.330 --> 00:55:50.460
because it's so beautiful

00:55:50.460 --> 00:55:53.410
and makes you feel so good in the morning,

00:55:53.410 --> 00:55:56.470
but in return for that gift
that the bird was given,

00:55:56.470 --> 00:56:00.330
it has a responsibility
to sing up the sun,

00:56:00.330 --> 00:56:04.160
so the gift comes with
a responsibility, right?

00:56:04.160 --> 00:56:05.040
So that's what we mean.

00:56:05.040 --> 00:56:08.590
The stars were given
this gift of twinkling

00:56:08.590 --> 00:56:10.160
and the responsibility

00:56:10.160 --> 00:56:13.470
to show us our directions at night.

00:56:13.470 --> 00:56:18.470
Everything comes in this duality
of gift and responsibility,

00:56:18.500 --> 00:56:20.190
and when we think about

00:56:20.190 --> 00:56:25.000
the difficulties that face us

00:56:25.000 --> 00:56:27.090
in this particular time,

00:56:27.090 --> 00:56:30.017
we could ask ourselves with heavy heart,

00:56:30.017 --> 00:56:32.007
"What's my responsibility?"

00:56:34.600 --> 00:56:37.490
And sometimes, that's harder to answer,

00:56:37.490 --> 00:56:40.820
but what if you asked
yourself, "What is my gift?"

00:56:40.820 --> 00:56:41.913
What is my gift?

00:56:43.210 --> 00:56:44.370
And then that becomes,

00:56:44.370 --> 00:56:46.913
giving your gift becomes
the responsibility,

00:56:48.140 --> 00:56:50.780
and is your gift as a teacher?

00:56:50.780 --> 00:56:53.350
As a scientist? As an artist?

00:56:53.350 --> 00:56:56.040
As a writer? As a parent?

00:56:56.040 --> 00:56:58.100
As a gardener? As an engineer?

00:56:58.100 --> 00:56:59.290
What is your gift?

00:56:59.290 --> 00:57:01.270
And the notion of this coupling

00:57:01.270 --> 00:57:03.550
of gifts and responsibilities

00:57:03.550 --> 00:57:05.930
is that in return for the gifts

00:57:05.930 --> 00:57:08.370
that we are given by the earth,

00:57:08.370 --> 00:57:12.523
that each of us is called upon
to give our gift in return,

00:57:13.640 --> 00:57:16.130
so that collectively we might do

00:57:16.130 --> 00:57:20.320
what Onondaga Clan
Mother Audrey Shenandoah

00:57:20.320 --> 00:57:21.560
beseeched us to do,

00:57:21.560 --> 00:57:24.343
and that is to seek justice,

00:57:25.280 --> 00:57:30.113
not only for ourselves, but
justice for all creation.

00:57:32.085 --> 00:57:34.807
(speaks in Potawatomi),
thank you very much.

00:57:34.807 --> 00:57:37.974
(audience applauding)

00:57:46.003 --> 00:57:46.836
Thank you.

00:57:50.378 --> 00:57:52.083
- [Man] You have an
opportunity for some questions-

00:57:52.083 --> 00:57:52.916
- Sure.

00:57:52.916 --> 00:57:54.730
(man speaks indistinctly)

00:57:54.730 --> 00:57:56.140
You bet. You bet.

00:57:56.140 --> 00:57:57.400
Any questions?

00:57:57.400 --> 00:58:02.400
Any questions or comments?
(man speaks indistinctly)

00:58:10.535 --> 00:58:12.118
Oh, here comes one.

00:58:13.280 --> 00:58:14.113
- [Audience Member] Thank you, Robin.

00:58:14.113 --> 00:58:16.290
It was a beautiful talk. John Grimm.

00:58:16.290 --> 00:58:19.290
I recall in an article by Winona LaDuke

00:58:20.740 --> 00:58:22.100
that she opened it

00:58:22.100 --> 00:58:23.927
by talking about (speaks
in foreign language),

00:58:26.150 --> 00:58:28.930
and she translated it or gave it

00:58:28.930 --> 00:58:33.210
as long life, happiness,
living into old age,

00:58:33.210 --> 00:58:34.363
and I caught,

00:58:35.390 --> 00:58:37.990
among your names of the
plant, (speaks tentatively)-

00:58:39.887 --> 00:58:40.962
- Bmaadiziaki,

00:58:40.962 --> 00:58:41.810
uh-huh, yeah.
- So I was looking

00:58:41.810 --> 00:58:46.810
for the relationship between the Ojibwe

00:58:47.160 --> 00:58:50.550
rendition of a value in life,

00:58:50.550 --> 00:58:52.930
a primary, central value in life,

00:58:52.930 --> 00:58:56.320
and here this value is then
embedded in plants, huh?

00:58:56.320 --> 00:58:59.350
- Yes, and to clarify,

00:58:59.350 --> 00:59:02.130
Ojibwe and Potawatomi
are very close languages,

00:59:02.130 --> 00:59:04.410
member of the same language family,

00:59:04.410 --> 00:59:07.200
and the notion of (speaks in Potawatomi)

00:59:07.200 --> 00:59:12.200
means a good and full life.

00:59:12.240 --> 00:59:13.990
It has many nuances

00:59:13.990 --> 00:59:17.450
of what does it mean to
have a good and full life,

00:59:17.450 --> 00:59:21.833
but it literally means to walk
in a good way on the earth,

00:59:22.880 --> 00:59:25.817
and so the notion of bmaadiziaki

00:59:27.380 --> 00:59:28.750
that I've used here

00:59:28.750 --> 00:59:30.920
is one who is living

00:59:30.920 --> 00:59:34.130
that (speaks in Potawatomi), on the earth,

00:59:34.130 --> 00:59:34.963
so yeah.

00:59:34.963 --> 00:59:35.796
- [Audience Member] Plants
are (speaks faintly)

00:59:35.796 --> 00:59:36.629
in that way also.

00:59:36.629 --> 00:59:40.063
- Yes, yeah, yeah. Good question.

00:59:47.040 --> 00:59:48.440
- [Amy] Thank you very much.

00:59:53.300 --> 00:59:54.580
I can feel very moved.

00:59:54.580 --> 00:59:58.530
I can feel very close to
so many of the concepts

00:59:58.530 --> 01:00:02.030
that you were talking about today.

01:00:02.030 --> 01:00:05.176
I feel that my culture is so far away from

01:00:05.176 --> 01:00:06.463
(sobs) jeez,

01:00:07.340 --> 01:00:10.570
so I'm wondering how do we take
the steps, little or small,

01:00:10.570 --> 01:00:15.470
to begin to turn that around
as individuals in this society?

01:00:15.470 --> 01:00:17.450
- Yeah, Amy, I-

01:00:17.450 --> 01:00:18.283
- [Amy] Sorry.

01:00:18.283 --> 01:00:20.000
- No, Amy, I honor your

01:00:21.510 --> 01:00:23.320
emotion about that

01:00:24.610 --> 01:00:26.080
because it is a grief.

01:00:26.080 --> 01:00:29.140
It is a grief to know
that we have this world

01:00:29.140 --> 01:00:30.757
of these beings who are sustaining us

01:00:30.757 --> 01:00:33.700
and the way we treat
those who take care of us

01:00:33.700 --> 01:00:36.440
in such a, well, such a way

01:00:36.440 --> 01:00:37.460
is a source of grief,

01:00:37.460 --> 01:00:40.490
and I really think about
Joanna Macy's wonderful work

01:00:40.490 --> 01:00:45.120
of understanding that our
grief is a measure of our love,

01:00:45.120 --> 01:00:46.503
and that, to me,

01:00:47.420 --> 01:00:50.560
as we try to heal this great rift

01:00:50.560 --> 01:00:53.493
of objectification of the living world,

01:00:54.700 --> 01:00:56.740
I think the notion of paying attention

01:00:56.740 --> 01:00:58.500
is really, really important,

01:00:58.500 --> 01:01:02.100
and not relegating, paying
attention to living beings,

01:01:02.100 --> 01:01:04.140
to nature over there,

01:01:04.140 --> 01:01:08.240
that we have to go to
rainforests of Ecuador

01:01:08.240 --> 01:01:09.270
to know nature,

01:01:09.270 --> 01:01:12.273
that we have to go to the
Adirondacks to know nature,

01:01:13.970 --> 01:01:17.760
that our regard for living
beings are in our garden

01:01:17.760 --> 01:01:19.680
and in the cracks of our sidewalk.

01:01:19.680 --> 01:01:20.830
Pay attention.

01:01:20.830 --> 01:01:24.150
Pay attention, and I think
that all of these things

01:01:24.150 --> 01:01:26.480
that could transform the way that we are

01:01:26.480 --> 01:01:27.650
are our human gifts.

01:01:27.650 --> 01:01:29.490
We don't have to invent this

01:01:29.490 --> 01:01:31.730
because we're really good at gratitude.

01:01:31.730 --> 01:01:34.300
We're really good at gift-giving,

01:01:34.300 --> 01:01:39.110
and when we turn those to
living beings around us,

01:01:39.110 --> 01:01:41.000
then we really have something.

01:01:41.000 --> 01:01:42.850
We don't have to invent something new.

01:01:42.850 --> 01:01:45.060
We have to extend our human,

01:01:45.060 --> 01:01:48.270
the compassion that we feel sometimes

01:01:48.270 --> 01:01:50.760
for some humans to the living world.

01:01:50.760 --> 01:01:53.200
- [Amy] But then, maybe as a followup,

01:01:53.200 --> 01:01:57.113
I can see us individually
committing to that and doing that.

01:01:59.200 --> 01:02:00.500
Do you have any insights

01:02:00.500 --> 01:02:04.730
as to how we create greater
change in our entire society

01:02:04.730 --> 01:02:07.660
in the consumerism that we do embody

01:02:07.660 --> 01:02:09.103
that we see all around us?

01:02:10.320 --> 01:02:11.930
Our governance and things like that?

01:02:11.930 --> 01:02:12.763
How do we,

01:02:13.710 --> 01:02:17.220
do you have thoughts on how
that can become big enough

01:02:17.220 --> 01:02:19.610
so that there is systemic change?

01:02:19.610 --> 01:02:21.810
- Yeah, of course,

01:02:21.810 --> 01:02:24.060
like probably every
one of us in this room,

01:02:24.060 --> 01:02:26.840
we wrestle with exactly that question

01:02:26.840 --> 01:02:29.930
of how do we scale this
to worldview change?

01:02:29.930 --> 01:02:32.500
Because that is what we're doing.

01:02:32.500 --> 01:02:34.150
We have to scale it,

01:02:34.150 --> 01:02:35.073
and one of the things

01:02:35.073 --> 01:02:37.577
that I have found important to talk about,

01:02:37.577 --> 01:02:42.090
and the reason that I spend a
great deal of time and effort,

01:02:42.090 --> 01:02:44.270
both writing and teaching and speaking

01:02:44.270 --> 01:02:47.210
about Indigenous knowledge systems

01:02:47.210 --> 01:02:50.280
is because I think we have
somehow gotten trapped

01:02:50.280 --> 01:02:53.000
into the notion that the relationship,

01:02:53.000 --> 01:02:55.090
the damaged relationship
that you're talking about,

01:02:55.090 --> 01:02:56.380
the one that brings us grief

01:02:56.380 --> 01:02:59.030
through our estrangement
from the living world,

01:02:59.030 --> 01:03:01.420
we tend to think that that's our default,

01:03:01.420 --> 01:03:02.730
that that's just the way it is,

01:03:02.730 --> 01:03:05.860
that's just the way it is for humanity,

01:03:05.860 --> 01:03:07.410
but what we have forgotten

01:03:07.410 --> 01:03:10.260
is that it's really
just an eyeblink of time

01:03:10.260 --> 01:03:13.460
that we have been in this
objectified worldview.

01:03:13.460 --> 01:03:16.570
For most of human history,

01:03:16.570 --> 01:03:20.690
we have lived with notions
of gratitude and reciprocity

01:03:20.690 --> 01:03:22.793
and lived well and richly.

01:03:24.020 --> 01:03:26.500
It is if we have been doing an experiment

01:03:26.500 --> 01:03:28.240
since the Industrial Revolution

01:03:28.240 --> 01:03:29.770
of what would it be like

01:03:29.770 --> 01:03:34.523
if we treated the world as
if it was it instead of you?

01:03:35.760 --> 01:03:38.340
Instead of ki? Instead of kin?

01:03:38.340 --> 01:03:40.727
We've been doing an experiment to say,

01:03:40.727 --> 01:03:43.500
"What if we treated the
world as if it was dead?"

01:03:43.500 --> 01:03:46.000
And the experiments, or the data from,

01:03:46.000 --> 01:03:49.010
the results from those
experiments are in, right?

01:03:49.010 --> 01:03:50.650
And we don't like them,

01:03:50.650 --> 01:03:54.900
and so I think it's really
important to know that

01:03:54.900 --> 01:03:57.210
and to speak about that,

01:03:57.210 --> 01:03:59.080
that there are other ways to be

01:03:59.080 --> 01:04:02.770
that are coherent, long products

01:04:02.770 --> 01:04:06.600
of cultural evolution in place,

01:04:06.600 --> 01:04:08.420
and there are so many tools.

01:04:08.420 --> 01:04:11.620
I mean, when we talk about
being there, being on the land,

01:04:11.620 --> 01:04:15.540
paying attention, conversations,

01:04:15.540 --> 01:04:16.690
stories, science,

01:04:16.690 --> 01:04:19.670
all the gifts that every one
of you in this room possess

01:04:19.670 --> 01:04:22.503
if deployed toward this notion,

01:04:23.970 --> 01:04:27.920
I think can be very powerful.

01:04:27.920 --> 01:04:30.350
It's not gonna happen from the top.

01:04:30.350 --> 01:04:33.350
(audience chuckles)

01:04:38.710 --> 01:04:40.490
- [Rayna] Hi. Thanks.

01:04:40.490 --> 01:04:42.313
I'm Rayna. Thank you for your talk.

01:04:43.460 --> 01:04:44.293
I was wondering.

01:04:44.293 --> 01:04:45.126
I was very intrigued

01:04:45.126 --> 01:04:46.670
by your thoughts on language and culture,

01:04:46.670 --> 01:04:48.710
sort of what you alluded to just now,

01:04:48.710 --> 01:04:50.370
and I'm from Greece,

01:04:50.370 --> 01:04:54.150
so in Greek language, we do
have gender for everything.

01:04:54.150 --> 01:04:56.970
We don't say it, pretty much for anything,

01:04:56.970 --> 01:04:59.430
except for a few neutral things,

01:04:59.430 --> 01:05:01.140
but I regret to say that doesn't make us

01:05:01.140 --> 01:05:05.963
any more nicer to the earth
or thoughtful or anything.

01:05:06.860 --> 01:05:08.140
What do you think it's gonna take,

01:05:08.140 --> 01:05:10.660
other than changing the language,

01:05:10.660 --> 01:05:13.370
in terms of getting people
to actually see that,

01:05:13.370 --> 01:05:16.380
and what are the other
tools that we can use?

01:05:16.380 --> 01:05:19.120
'Cause I feel like language
might be one small part of it,

01:05:19.120 --> 01:05:20.603
but it will take a lot more.

01:05:21.490 --> 01:05:23.130
- Absolutely. Absolutely.

01:05:23.130 --> 01:05:24.877
People have brought me
so many examples of,

01:05:24.877 --> 01:05:26.597
"Wait, we have animacy in our language,

01:05:26.597 --> 01:05:28.210
"and yeah, we're not doing so well,"

01:05:28.210 --> 01:05:29.953
so it certainly isn't,

01:05:30.979 --> 01:05:34.440
and we're not talking
about deterministic here.

01:05:34.440 --> 01:05:36.570
It is often correlated,

01:05:36.570 --> 01:05:38.910
but it's certainly not deterministic.

01:05:38.910 --> 01:05:41.910
One of my students actually
had an interesting insight

01:05:41.910 --> 01:05:46.160
about this notion of the changed pronouns.

01:05:46.160 --> 01:05:50.187
She said, "Do you remember
that we used to say,

01:05:50.187 --> 01:05:52.767
"when the fire truck
would go by, we would say,

01:05:52.767 --> 01:05:57.767
"'Well, the firemen are
going to put out the fire.'"

01:05:58.170 --> 01:05:59.593
But do we say that anymore?

01:06:01.920 --> 01:06:03.513
We say firefighters, don't we?

01:06:04.600 --> 01:06:06.560
A cultural shift has occurred,

01:06:06.560 --> 01:06:08.860
a little cultural shift in language,

01:06:08.860 --> 01:06:12.000
and we pause, and we say, "firefighter"

01:06:12.000 --> 01:06:13.760
because in that pause,

01:06:13.760 --> 01:06:17.610
we're saying both men and women can be,

01:06:17.610 --> 01:06:19.220
can put out blazes,

01:06:19.220 --> 01:06:23.783
and just in that pause of
choosing to use a different word,

01:06:25.030 --> 01:06:28.620
it reflects change and it propels change,

01:06:28.620 --> 01:06:29.710
and so this notion

01:06:31.350 --> 01:06:35.160
of pausing before saying
it about living beings

01:06:35.160 --> 01:06:37.440
could have some of that same effect,

01:06:37.440 --> 01:06:40.600
but it's about so much more than language.

01:06:40.600 --> 01:06:43.400
What we really need is a change of hearts,

01:06:43.400 --> 01:06:47.490
and this is why, for me, as a scientist,

01:06:47.490 --> 01:06:51.340
I feel like I'm, well, I
don't think we need more data.

01:06:51.340 --> 01:06:53.960
I don't think we need more information.

01:06:53.960 --> 01:06:56.240
I don't think we need
to change technology.

01:06:56.240 --> 01:06:58.490
We need to change our hearts,

01:06:58.490 --> 01:07:01.710
but truthfully, I think
that we already have that,

01:07:01.710 --> 01:07:04.040
that we have that innate sense

01:07:04.040 --> 01:07:07.800
of wonder and love and
beauty for the natural world,

01:07:07.800 --> 01:07:08.970
but we have to let it out.

01:07:08.970 --> 01:07:10.630
We have to free it,

01:07:10.630 --> 01:07:12.550
and there's all these things around us

01:07:12.550 --> 01:07:15.210
that push us down so that we can't,

01:07:15.210 --> 01:07:16.640
but we have to just choose it.

01:07:16.640 --> 01:07:17.713
We have to choose it.

01:07:22.410 --> 01:07:25.320
- [Audience Member] Maybe
a partial hopeful answer

01:07:25.320 --> 01:07:27.480
about how we can.

01:07:27.480 --> 01:07:28.313
Sorry.

01:07:28.313 --> 01:07:30.470
Maybe a partial hopeful answer

01:07:30.470 --> 01:07:34.313
about how we can start to
move the society a little bit.

01:07:35.880 --> 01:07:37.330
I thought I was the only one.

01:07:37.330 --> 01:07:40.580
I thought, in fact, that my recent

01:07:40.580 --> 01:07:45.150
and incredibly increased
attention toward plants.

01:07:45.150 --> 01:07:46.930
I used to, when my parents were alive,

01:07:46.930 --> 01:07:49.620
be polite about their gardens,

01:07:49.620 --> 01:07:51.740
but I wasn't paying attention.

01:07:51.740 --> 01:07:53.310
After they died,

01:07:53.310 --> 01:07:58.030
I got so mind-bogglingly
amazed at what plants do

01:07:58.030 --> 01:08:00.630
that I thought I was being haunted,

01:08:00.630 --> 01:08:02.870
but anyway,

01:08:02.870 --> 01:08:06.060
then I realized, as I read around a bit,

01:08:06.060 --> 01:08:07.910
this is a common pattern

01:08:07.910 --> 01:08:12.830
that people, as they age,
frequently do get this way,

01:08:12.830 --> 01:08:15.720
and I remembered that
the Voltaire "Candide"

01:08:15.720 --> 01:08:18.710
at the end of that whole story,

01:08:18.710 --> 01:08:21.720
what Candide and Cunegonde do is

01:08:21.720 --> 01:08:24.840
tend their garden into old age,

01:08:24.840 --> 01:08:29.840
so we have an avalanche
of aging people here.

01:08:29.990 --> 01:08:31.060
I think it's time

01:08:31.060 --> 01:08:34.670
to start to push paying
attention to plants

01:08:34.670 --> 01:08:37.720
as a really fine thing

01:08:37.720 --> 01:08:39.990
that can help heal the society,

01:08:39.990 --> 01:08:43.090
and the sense of gratitude,
as I understand it,

01:08:43.090 --> 01:08:46.573
is a huge key to happy aging.

01:08:47.790 --> 01:08:49.660
- Well said, well said,

01:08:49.660 --> 01:08:50.910
and time in the garden

01:08:50.910 --> 01:08:55.370
is a perfect place to
understand plants as beings

01:08:55.370 --> 01:08:58.800
and plants as gifts, and
our relationship to them.

01:08:58.800 --> 01:09:02.930
Yeah, both the way

01:09:02.930 --> 01:09:05.700
that you phrased your question
and your question itself

01:09:06.550 --> 01:09:10.150
makes me think of a way to
pull together this question

01:09:10.150 --> 01:09:12.080
that none of us have the answer to of

01:09:12.080 --> 01:09:13.623
how do we change our world?

01:09:14.640 --> 01:09:16.820
But again, I wanna come
back to this notion of

01:09:16.820 --> 01:09:21.170
what we already know that
we don't have to invent,

01:09:21.170 --> 01:09:23.980
and I've come to see my work,

01:09:23.980 --> 01:09:27.780
both as a scientist, as
a writer, as a teacher,

01:09:27.780 --> 01:09:32.380
and as a parent and a
grandparent, as a human being,

01:09:32.380 --> 01:09:36.140
is to help people, again,

01:09:36.140 --> 01:09:37.940
come to fall in love with the world,

01:09:39.780 --> 01:09:43.763
and when we fall in love with
the world, all else follows,

01:09:44.810 --> 01:09:46.760
and a garden is a great place to begin.

01:09:54.640 --> 01:09:56.653
- [Audience Member] Hello. Thank you.

01:09:58.040 --> 01:10:00.050
I am struck that

01:10:01.750 --> 01:10:06.430
the way I heard your description
of the Honorable Harvest

01:10:06.430 --> 01:10:07.340
works really well

01:10:07.340 --> 01:10:11.653
for very low-impact
gathering and maybe hunting,

01:10:12.740 --> 01:10:15.160
but anytime we're doing something else,

01:10:15.160 --> 01:10:19.420
even the most ecologically
sensitive polyculture farming

01:10:19.420 --> 01:10:21.727
or something like

01:10:23.510 --> 01:10:25.150
selectively burning certain areas

01:10:25.150 --> 01:10:29.143
to promote the growth of
particular species there,

01:10:30.070 --> 01:10:31.663
that's us saying,

01:10:33.899 --> 01:10:36.690
"We're choosing what lives there," right?

01:10:36.690 --> 01:10:39.310
When we move around, when we live places,

01:10:39.310 --> 01:10:42.270
we have these bubbles of
controlled ecologies around us,

01:10:42.270 --> 01:10:47.210
and in that,

01:10:47.210 --> 01:10:48.043
any of those,

01:10:48.043 --> 01:10:50.860
and these comes in many different ways,

01:10:50.860 --> 01:10:52.607
that's us selectively saying,

01:10:52.607 --> 01:10:54.357
"We're going to destroy what's here

01:10:55.267 --> 01:10:56.307
"and put something else there

01:10:56.307 --> 01:10:58.600
"or allow something else to grow there,"

01:10:58.600 --> 01:11:03.130
and unless you invoke

01:11:03.130 --> 01:11:05.120
metaphysics, which I'm
not averse to doing;

01:11:05.120 --> 01:11:06.320
I am a religious person,

01:11:08.090 --> 01:11:11.420
the rest of the world would
be at least as better off,

01:11:11.420 --> 01:11:12.803
if not much better off,

01:11:15.100 --> 01:11:17.330
without our participation,

01:11:17.330 --> 01:11:18.660
and so

01:11:23.300 --> 01:11:26.190
I am coming to a place of nihilism

01:11:26.190 --> 01:11:30.010
that this kind of

01:11:30.010 --> 01:11:31.310
reciprocal relationship

01:11:31.310 --> 01:11:36.310
is something that is ever
attainable at anywhere.

01:11:36.330 --> 01:11:40.590
- Yeah, I hear your question,

01:11:40.590 --> 01:11:42.933
and there's so much more wrapped up in it.

01:11:43.920 --> 01:11:47.450
The only tiny little story,

01:11:47.450 --> 01:11:49.570
slides that I didn't get to,

01:11:49.570 --> 01:11:53.510
is we asked ourselves
exactly that question of

01:11:53.510 --> 01:11:57.580
how can we take in such a way

01:11:57.580 --> 01:12:01.560
that promotes life,

01:12:01.560 --> 01:12:04.300
promotes regeneration, in a sense,

01:12:04.300 --> 01:12:07.750
under the agency of the plants?

01:12:07.750 --> 01:12:09.563
Can we have a true mutualism?

01:12:10.520 --> 01:12:13.090
And let me just tell you
this tiny little story.

01:12:13.090 --> 01:12:15.070
It is about sweetgrass.

01:12:15.070 --> 01:12:18.220
We did some experimental
work with sweetgrass,

01:12:18.220 --> 01:12:22.740
which is harvested by basket
makers and Native people,

01:12:22.740 --> 01:12:27.683
and I'm trying to think of
how to abbreviate this story.

01:12:28.610 --> 01:12:30.623
The plant was declining,

01:12:31.840 --> 01:12:33.810
and so one of the questions

01:12:33.810 --> 01:12:36.350
that the basket makers
came to us at the center

01:12:36.350 --> 01:12:39.180
asking for some help
in understanding this,

01:12:39.180 --> 01:12:42.140
and because the plant was declining,

01:12:42.140 --> 01:12:45.960
what does Western conservation
biology tells us we must do

01:12:45.960 --> 01:12:48.853
when that plant is becoming endangered?

01:12:49.870 --> 01:12:51.310
We have to leave it alone, right?

01:12:51.310 --> 01:12:53.410
Because nature and people are a bad mix.

01:12:53.410 --> 01:12:54.413
We have to stay away from that.

01:12:54.413 --> 01:12:56.920
We have to stop harvesting the sweetgrass

01:12:56.920 --> 01:12:58.193
so that it can recover.

01:12:59.100 --> 01:13:01.960
The basket makers, the
Indigenous basket makers,

01:13:01.960 --> 01:13:03.850
told us something a little bit different.

01:13:03.850 --> 01:13:06.400
They told us a lot of
different, a lot of information,

01:13:06.400 --> 01:13:09.950
but one of the things that
we learned from them is

01:13:09.950 --> 01:13:14.740
we evaluated the response
of sweetgrass to harvesting,

01:13:14.740 --> 01:13:17.910
and so we had a plot
where we didn't take any,

01:13:17.910 --> 01:13:19.740
and we know what would
happen there, right?

01:13:19.740 --> 01:13:20.940
The sweetgrass would flourish

01:13:20.940 --> 01:13:23.230
because we weren't messing with it,

01:13:23.230 --> 01:13:24.730
and then we had other plots

01:13:24.730 --> 01:13:26.720
where we harvested the sweetgrass

01:13:26.720 --> 01:13:28.703
according to the Honorable Harvest,

01:13:29.540 --> 01:13:31.750
and then we went and measured,

01:13:31.750 --> 01:13:35.350
used all our plant ecology
tools to measure the success,

01:13:35.350 --> 01:13:36.850
and do you know what happened?

01:13:38.481 --> 01:13:41.590
The plots of plants that
had no human intervention,

01:13:41.590 --> 01:13:44.560
that had no harvesters
there singing to them,

01:13:44.560 --> 01:13:48.800
taking their, receiving their
gifts and reciprocating,

01:13:48.800 --> 01:13:50.120
the control plots

01:13:50.120 --> 01:13:53.283
that Western science said
should thrive declined.

01:13:54.290 --> 01:13:55.780
The plots that were harvested

01:13:55.780 --> 01:14:00.243
according to the Honorable
Harvest doubled in their vigor.

01:14:01.720 --> 01:14:04.070
What we saw was that

01:14:04.070 --> 01:14:07.010
practice of the Honorable
Harvest with sweetgrass

01:14:07.010 --> 01:14:10.450
was a stimulus to the
growth of those plants

01:14:10.450 --> 01:14:13.260
in taking and accepting the gift

01:14:13.260 --> 01:14:15.450
and reciprocating with care,

01:14:15.450 --> 01:14:17.450
the plants then regenerated,

01:14:17.450 --> 01:14:20.700
so there are models out
there of true mutualism,

01:14:20.700 --> 01:14:24.580
true reciprocity among beings,

01:14:24.580 --> 01:14:27.780
and I don't want to
oversimplify this in any way

01:14:27.780 --> 01:14:29.430
because there are plenty of plants

01:14:29.430 --> 01:14:31.980
that wouldn't respond in that same way,

01:14:31.980 --> 01:14:34.700
but I hold that up as an example of

01:14:34.700 --> 01:14:37.097
when we shift our frame to say,

01:14:37.097 --> 01:14:40.567
"Well, what are the ways
in which humans and plants

01:14:40.567 --> 01:14:43.280
"could be mutually
beneficial to one another?"

01:14:43.280 --> 01:14:44.550
we find them,

01:14:44.550 --> 01:14:48.080
and working with Native communities,

01:14:48.080 --> 01:14:49.430
really around the world,

01:14:49.430 --> 01:14:51.640
listening to them talk about this concept,

01:14:51.640 --> 01:14:53.580
there are so many examples

01:14:53.580 --> 01:14:56.283
of plants that flourish
when people harvest them,

01:14:57.160 --> 01:14:59.913
and don't diminish if you
use the Honorable Harvest.

01:15:01.530 --> 01:15:03.490
A long answer, but a good question.

01:15:03.490 --> 01:15:05.150
Yes.
- One last question.

01:15:05.150 --> 01:15:06.406
- [Audience Member] Are
you heartened at all?

01:15:06.406 --> 01:15:09.580
First of all, thank you.
That was a wonderful talk.

01:15:09.580 --> 01:15:11.720
Are you heartened at all by trends

01:15:11.720 --> 01:15:15.500
like the locavore food
movement and community gardens,

01:15:15.500 --> 01:15:17.040
especially in poor,

01:15:17.040 --> 01:15:20.660
underserved communities?
- Absolutely. Absolutely.

01:15:20.660 --> 01:15:23.180
To me, that is one of the most important,

01:15:23.180 --> 01:15:25.340
and as you say, heartening examples.

01:15:25.340 --> 01:15:28.270
When the local food movement began,

01:15:28.270 --> 01:15:30.410
I was a little skeptical about it.

01:15:30.410 --> 01:15:32.450
It seemed like it was coming

01:15:32.450 --> 01:15:35.970
from a place of great
privilege and so forth,

01:15:35.970 --> 01:15:38.720
but what I'm seeing in
the local food movement

01:15:38.720 --> 01:15:42.860
is that it is reawakening our relationship

01:15:42.860 --> 01:15:45.040
to land, to soil, to water,

01:15:45.040 --> 01:15:47.513
and it's cutting across all borders,

01:15:48.370 --> 01:15:49.570
from urban gardens

01:15:49.570 --> 01:15:53.070
to the Indigenous food
sovereignty movement

01:15:54.170 --> 01:15:59.120
to anti-Monsanto kinds
of political movements,

01:15:59.120 --> 01:16:03.630
so yeah, I think the food
movement is a huge indicator

01:16:03.630 --> 01:16:08.450
and maybe even a tool of reshaping
our relationship to earth

01:16:08.450 --> 01:16:09.883
'cause everybody eats.

01:16:12.019 --> 01:16:12.987
- [Man] Thank you very much.

01:16:12.987 --> 01:16:14.463
- Thanks.
(audience applauding)

01:16:14.463 --> 01:16:15.296
Thank you.

01:16:21.211 --> 01:16:23.961
(tranquil music)

